{"id": "82", "question": "symptoms and causes of ibs", "gt": "Symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, constipation alternating with diarrhea, belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement, and a lot of gas or bloating. Causes of irritable bowel syndrome are unknown but it is believed to be due to a number of factors including alteration in gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.", "pred": "Causes of IBS are thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, and constipation alternating with diarrhea. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\n\nCauses of IBS are thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.", "cot": "[\"Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, and constipation alternating with diarrhea. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nCauses of IBS are thought to result from a combination of abnormal gastrointestinal (GI) tract movements, increased awareness of bodily functions, and a disruption in the communication between the brain and the GI tract. It is also believed to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal (GI) tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances.\", \"Based on the provided passages, the symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) include diarrhea, constipation, and alternating diarrhea and constipation. Additionally, people with IBS often experience belly pains or cramps, which tend to worsen after meals and improve after a bowel movement. They may also have a lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nThe causes of IBS are not fully understood, but it is believed to result from a combination of factors. These include alterations in the gastrointestinal tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food intolerances. The exact cause of IBS is still unknown.\", \"Symptoms of IBS include diarrhea (often described as violent episodes of diarrhea), constipation, and diarrhea alternating with constipation. Belly pains or cramps, usually in the lower half of the belly, that get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. A lot of gas or bloating.\\n\\nCauses of IBS are thought to be due to a number of factors, including alteration in the gastrointestinal tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. The following are risk factors thought to cause IBS: 1. Abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong) 2. Hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas. 3. Food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\", \"Symptoms of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) can include diarrhea, constipation, or alternating constipation and diarrhea. People with IBS may also experience belly pains or cramps, often in the lower half of the belly, which tend to get worse after meals and feel better after a bowel movement. Additionally, there is often a lot of gas or bloating. \\n\\nCauses of irritable bowel syndrome are not fully understood but are believed to involve a combination of factors. These include alterations in gastrointestinal tract motility, abnormal nervous system signals, increased sensitivity to pain, and food intolerances. Risk factors thought to contribute to IBS include abnormal movements of the colon and small intestines (too fast or slow, or too strong), hypersensitivity to pain from a full bowel or gas, and food sensitivities, possibly caused by poor absorption of sugars or acids in food.\"]", "time_sec": 22.98, "generation_time_seconds": 22.9743, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0007, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [27912, 51173, 315, 358, 7347, 2924, 68293, 320, 60817, 7481, 438, 16401, 17770, 315, 68293, 701, 733, 48586, 11, 323, 733, 48586, 72362, 448, 68293, 13, 96585, 50166, 476, 1560, 14647, 11, 5990, 304, 279, 4722, 4279, 315, 279, 35417, 11, 429, 633, 10960, 1283, 20969, 323, 2666, 2664, 1283, 264, 65258, 7203, 13, 362, 2696, 315, 6819, 476, 14211, 1095, 382, 22571, 4776, 315, 358, 7347, 525, 3381, 311, 1102, 504, 264, 10601, 315, 34563, 79211, 320, 28034, 8, 41829, 19029, 11, 7172, 17526, 315, 47031, 5746, 11, 323, 264, 43119, 304, 279, 10535, 1948, 279, 8109, 323, 279, 48509, 41829, 13, 1084, 374, 1083, 11585, 311, 387, 4152, 311, 264, 1372, 315, 9363, 11, 2670, 72581, 304, 279, 79211, 320, 28034, 8, 41829, 3852, 1403, 11, 34563, 22596, 1849, 17289, 11, 7172, 26443, 311, 6646, 11, 323, 3607, 71629, 3020