{"id": "92", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Essential amino acids are those that cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be made by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Some examples of nonessential amino acids are alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.", "pred": "Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained through the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To determine the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids, let's analyze the information provided in the passages:\n\n1. Essential Amino Acids:\n   - Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained through the diet.\n   - Adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan.\n   - The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants.\n\n2. Nonessential Amino Acids:\n   - Nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\n   - These amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\n\n3. Function:\n   - Essential amino acids are necessary for the body to build proteins, which are crucial for cell repair, growth, and maintenance.\n   - Nonessential amino acids can also be used by the body to build proteins, but they are not essential for this purpose because they can be synthesized or obtained from other sources.\n\nIn summary, the key difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\n\nAnswer: Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained through the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "cot": "[\"To determine the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids, let's analyze the information provided in the passages:\\n\\n1. Essential Amino Acids:\\n   - Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained through the diet.\\n   - Adults need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan.\\n   - The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants.\\n\\n2. Nonessential Amino Acids:\\n   - Nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\\n   - These amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\\n\\n3. Function:\\n   - Essential amino acids are necessary for the body to build proteins, which are crucial for cell repair, growth, and maintenance.\\n   - Nonessential amino acids can also be used by the body to build proteins, but they are not essential for this purpose because they can be synthesized or obtained from other sources.\\n\\nIn summary, the key difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\\n\\nAnswer: Essential amino acids are those that the human body cannot produce on its own and must be obtained through the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\", \"To determine the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids, let's analyze the information provided in the passages:\\n\\n1. **Essential Amino Acids**:\\n   - Essential amino acids are those that the body cannot produce and must be obtained through the diet.\\n   - There are nine essential amino acids for adults: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan.\\n   - These nine amino acids are crucial for the body to build proteins.\\n\\n2. **Nonessential Amino Acids**:\\n   - Nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\\n   - There are 11 nonessential amino acids, including alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\\n   - These acids are not necessary to obtain from the diet because the body can produce them on its own.\\n\\n3. **Function**:\\n   - Both essential and nonessential amino acids are the building blocks of proteins.\\n   - Essential amino acids are necessary for the body to build the wide variety of proteins it needs for repair, growth, and maintenance of cells.\\n\\nBased on this information, the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids lies in their necessity for the body to produce proteins:\\n- Essential amino acids must be obtained through the diet because the body cannot produce them.\\n- Nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body, so they do not need to be obtained from the diet.\\n\\nAnswer: Essential amino acids must be obtained through the diet because the body cannot produce them, while nonessential amino acids can be synthesized by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet.\", \"The difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, while essential amino acids cannot be made by the body so you must get them from your diet.\", \"Essential amino acids are necessary for the body and cannot be made by the body itself, so they must be obtained through the diet. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be produced by the body either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\"]", "time_sec": 30.274, "generation_time_seconds": 30.2654, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 8253, 279, 6672, 1948, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 11, 1077, 594, 23643, 279, 1995, 3897, 304, 279, 46769, 1447, 16, 13, 47733, 362, 31824, 6381, 3365, 510, 256, 481, 47733, 41400, 32869, 525, 1846, 429, 279, 3738, 2487, 4157, 8193, 389, 1181, 1828, 323, 1969, 387, 12180, 1526, 279, 9968, 624, 256, 481, 57238, 1184, 311, 6851, 8063, 7565, 41400, 32869, 25, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754, 482, 11, 512, 1754, 482, 11, 83395, 482, 11, 21068, 290, 482, 11, 14018, 3923, 32283, 482, 11, 270, 49790, 482, 11, 323, 1430, 417, 5127, 276, 624, 256, 481, 576, 42541, 7565, 41400, 13621, 11, 12744, 89973, 11, 374, 1172, 7565, 369, 41434, 382, 17, 13, 11581, 48482, 362, 31824, 6381, 3365, 510, 256, 481, 11581, 48482, 41400, 32869, 646, 387, 91006, 553, 279, 2487, 504, 1008, 41400, 32869, 476, 553, 14719, 1495, 12833, 3871, 624, 256, 481, 4220, 41400, 32869, 2924, 72562, 482, 11, 438, 1732, 23392, 11, 438, 4480, 292, 13621, 11, 34069, 3991, 13621, 11, 139