{"id": "141", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\nAccording to passage 1, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. This reduction is allowed under section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act.\n\nPassage 3 further states that the assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset (i.e., opening WDV of such asset less scrap value) even though such discarded asset is not used for the purposes of business or profession in such year and subsequent years.\n\nTherefore, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is to reduce the block value of the asset by the scrap value in the previous year in which it is discarded, and to claim depreciation on the residual value of the asset in the year of discard and subsequent years.", "pred": "**Conclusion**:\nWhen an asset is discarded and its value is reduced from the block based on the scrap value, the depreciation claim is made on the residual value of the asset, which is the difference between the opening WDV and the scrap value. This treatment aligns with the block concept under the Income Tax Act.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Based on the provided passages, the treatment when an asset is discarded under the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\n1. **Depreciation Block Concept**: Assets falling within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are grouped together and considered as a single asset.\n\n2. **Scrap Value Adjustment**: If an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset may be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. This adjustment is made according to the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\n\n3. **Depreciation on Discarded Asset**: The assessee (the person or entity filing the tax return) is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is the opening WDV (Written Down Value) of such asset less the scrap value.\n\n**Example**:\n- Suppose an asset with an opening WDV of Rs. 100,000 is discarded and has a scrap value of Rs. 20,000.\n- The depreciation claim for the discarded asset would be Rs. 100,000 - Rs. 20,000 = Rs. 80,000.\n\n**Conclusion**:\nWhen an asset is discarded and its value is reduced from the block based on the scrap value, the depreciation claim is made on the residual value of the asset, which is the difference between the opening WDV and the scrap value. This treatment aligns with the block concept under the Income Tax Act.", "cot": "[\"Based on the provided passages, the treatment when an asset is discarded under the Income Tax Act is as follows:\\n\\n1. **Depreciation Block Concept**: Assets falling within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are grouped together and considered as a single asset.\\n\\n2. **Scrap Value Adjustment**: If an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset may be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. This adjustment is made according to the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\\n\\n3. **Depreciation on Discarded Asset**: The assessee (the person or entity filing the tax return) is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is the opening WDV (Written Down Value) of such asset less the scrap value.\\n\\n**Example**:\\n- Suppose an asset with an opening WDV of Rs. 100,000 is discarded and has a scrap value of Rs. 20,000.\\n- The depreciation claim for the discarded asset would be Rs. 100,000 - Rs. 20,000 = Rs. 80,000.\\n\\n**Conclusion**:\\nWhen an asset is discarded and its value is reduced from the block based on the scrap value, the depreciation claim is made on the residual value of the asset, which is the difference between the opening WDV and the scrap value. This treatment aligns with the block concept under the Income Tax Act.\", \"To answer the question, let's break down the information provided in the passages:\\n\\n1. **Passage 1** mentions that under the block concept, all assets within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are considered as a single asset. When an asset is discarded but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year.\\n\\n2. **Passage 3** specifies that the assessee (the person or entity) is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of a discarded asset, even if the asset was not used for business or professional purposes in subsequent years. The residual value is defined as the opening WDV (Written Down Value) of the asset less its scrap value.\\n\\n3. **Passage 2** provides an illustration to clarify a standard related to depreciation. It does not directly address the treatment of an asset when discarded but provides context that the income before depreciation and tax remains consistent over the years.\\n\\nBased on these passages, the treatment for an asset discarded as per the Income Tax Act involves considering the asset's value under the block concept. If the asset is discarded but not sold, its scrap value can be reduced from the block in the previous year. The assessee can claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is the opening WDV of the asset less its scrap value.\\n\\nAnswer: When an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act, its scrap value can be reduced from the block in the previous year, and the asset's residual value (opening WDV less scrap value) can be claimed for depreciation, even if the asset was not used for business or professional purposes in subsequent years.\", \"Step 1: Identify the relevant information from the passages.\\n- Passage 1: \\\"In case an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, section 43(6) permits the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded.\\\"\\n- Passage 3: \\\"The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset [ie., Opening WDV of such asset less scrap value] even though such discarded asset is not used for the purposes of business or profession in such year and subsequent years.\\\"\\n\\nStep 2: Analyze the information.\\n- Both passages indicate that when an asset is discarded, the scrap value of the asset is considered for tax purposes.\\n- The scrap value is used to reduce the block (a unit of assets with the same rate of depreciation) in the previous year.\\n\\nStep 3: Formulate the answer based on the analysis.\\n- The treatment of an asset discarded as per the Income Tax Act involves reducing the block in the previous year by the scrap value of the asset.\\n\\nAnswer: When an asset is discarded, the scrap value of the asset is used to reduce the block in the previous year according to the Income Tax Act.\", \"When an asset is discarded, the treatment under the Income Tax Act is as follows:\\n\\n1. If the asset is not sold but discarded by the business, section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act allows the scrap value of the asset to be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded.\\n\\n2. The block concept is applied, where all assets falling within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are clubbed together and considered as a single asset.\\n\\n3. Any alterations to the value of the block must be made strictly in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\\n\\n4. If the discarded asset is not used for the purposes of business or profession in the year of discarding, the assessee is still entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset (i.e., the opening WDV of such asset less the scrap value).\\n\\nThese points summarize the treatment for an asset discarded under the Income Tax Act, considering the block concept and the provisions related to depreciation and scrap value.\"]", "time_sec": 37.224, "generation_time_seconds": 37.2196, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 3897, 46769, 11, 279, 6380, 979, 458, 9329, 374, 43210, 1212, 279, 32520, 15190, 3218, 374, 438, 11017, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 7839, 2758, 7101, 8362, 34355, 95518, 40638, 15679, 2878, 279, 1852, 536, 323, 3832, 311, 279, 1852, 4379, 315, 93089, 525, 40041, 3786, 323, 6509, 438, 264, 3175, 9329, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 3326, 4611, 5162, 91949, 95518, 1416, 458, 9329, 374, 43210, 553, 279, 2562, 714, 537, 6088, 11, 279, 20843, 897, 315, 279, 9329, 1231, 387, 11046, 504, 279, 2504, 304, 279, 3681, 1042, 304, 892, 1741, 9329, 374, 43210, 13, 1096, 27075, 374, 1865, 4092, 311, 279, 19156, 315, 15585, 16824, 422, 315, 279, 32520, 15190, 3218, 11, 220, 16, 24, 21, 16, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 7839, 2758, 7101, 389, 11735, 20958, 22605, 95518, 576, 79040, 4060, 320, 1782, 1697, 476, 5387, 25480, 279, 3742, 470, 8, 374, 19867, 311, 3717, 93089, 389, 279, 32147, 897, 315, 279, 43210, 9329, 11, 892, 374, 279, 8568, 467, 61971, 320, 35624, 6285, 5162, 8, 315, 1741, 9329, 2686, 279, 20843, 897, 382, 334, 13314, 334, 510, 12, 82610, 458, 9329, 448, 458, 8568, 467, 61971, 315, 19215, 13, 220, 16, 15, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 374, 43210, 323, 702, 264, 20843, 897, 315, 19215, 13, 220, 17, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 624, 12, 576, 93089, 3717, 369, 279, 43210, 9329, 1035, 387, 19215, 13, 220, 16, 15, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 481, 19215, 13, 220, 17, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 284, 19215, 13, 220, 23, 15, 11, 15, 15, 15, 382, 334, 43434, 334, 510, 4498, 458, 9329, 374, 43210, 323, 1181, 897, 