"pred": "To attach multiple picture frames together, start by laying the frames out on the floor to test the arrangement. Then, use hinges to join the frames, ensuring the hinge placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to ensure the grouped pictures stand securely on the table top. Screw each frame to a backer board and attach a hanging wire to the back of the assembled frames."}], "input_len": 547, "output_tokens": 975, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 11.85, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "207", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is what can be inferred about the vegetable \"chard\":\n\n* Chard is a type of beet (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) that has been chosen for leaf production over storage root formation.\n* Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\n* The flavor of chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive taste (some consider it bitter).\n* Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked to reduce bitterness and bring out a refined flavor.\n* Chard is an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden.\n* Chard is perishable and should be stored in the refrigerator to minimize spoilage.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"What is chard vegetable?\" is: A beet variety (Beta vulgaris subsp. maritima) grown for leaf production, characterized by large, fleshy leaves and stalks with a strong, bitter flavor that becomes more refined with cooking.", "pred": "Step 2: Synthesize the information to form a coherent answer.\nChard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It is characterized by its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, and it is typically harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the leaves have slightly tougher stems.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the key characteristics of chard from the passages.\n- Swiss chard is also known as just plain chard.\n- It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\n- Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor.\n- Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads.\n- Mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked.\n- Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation.\n- It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity.\n- Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored in the crisper for up to 2 to 3 days.\n\nStep 2: Synthesize the information to form a coherent answer.\nChard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It is characterized by its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, and it is typically harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the leaves have slightly tougher stems.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the key characteristics of chard from the passages.\\n- Swiss chard is also known as just plain chard.\\n- It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\\n- Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor.\\n- Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads.\\n- Mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked.\\n- Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation.\\n- It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity.\\n- Chard is extremely perishable and should be stored in the crisper for up to 2 to 3 days.\\n\\nStep 2: Synthesize the information to form a coherent answer.\\nChard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It is characterized by its large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, and it is typically harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the leaves have slightly tougher stems.\", \"Chard is a type of leafy green vegetable that is related to beets. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive, and some think, bitter flavor. Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads, while mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked, like in pizzoccheri, or sauteed. Their bitterness fades with cooking, leaving a refined flavor which is more delicate than that of cooked spinach. Chard can be harvested when the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stalks are larger and have slightly tougher stems.\", \"Step 1: Identify the key characteristics of chard described in the passages.\\n- Large, fleshy, tender deep green leaves\\n- Thick, crisp stalks\\n- Similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor\\n- Used fresh in salads or cooked\\n- Can be harvested while young or after maturity\\n- Extremely perishable, so keep refrigerated for a minimum of 2 to 3 days\\n\\nStep 2: Compare chard to other vegetables mentioned in the passages.\\n- Chard is related to beetroot and other beet cultivars, not spinach\\n- It has a stronger, more assertive flavor than spinach\\n\\nStep 3: Synthesize the information to determine what chard is.\\nChard is a leafy green vegetable that resembles spinach in appearance but has a stronger, more assertive flavor. It can be used fresh in salads or cooked, and is harvested while young or after maturity. Its leaves are large, fleshy, and tender, and its stalks are thick and crisp. Chard is extremely perishable, so it needs to be stored properly to maintain its quality.\\n\\nAnswer: Chard is a leafy green vegetable that resembles spinach in appearance but has a stronger, more assertive flavor. It can be used fresh in salads or cooked, and is harvested while young or after maturity. Its leaves are large, fleshy, and tender, and its stalks are thick and crisp. Chard is extremely perishable, so it needs to be stored properly to maintain its quality.\", \"Step 1: Identify the key characteristics of chard from the given passages.\\n- Chard has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\\n- Chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor.\\n- Chard can be used raw in salads or cooked.\\n- Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation.\\n- Chard produces fresh white, yellow or red leaf stalks.\\n\\nStep 2: Compare chard to other vegetables mentioned in the passages.\\n- Chard is similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive flavor.\\n- Chard has a flavor that is more delicate than cooked spinach.\\n- Chard is in the same subspecies as beetroot (garden beet) and all other beet cultivars.\\n\\nStep 3: Summarize the information to answer the question.\\nChard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach in appearance and can be used in salads or cooked, but it has a stronger, more assertive flavor. It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stalks are larger and slightly tougher. Chard is a popular ornamental vegetable that adds color to the vegetable garden.\\n\\nAnswer: Chard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach in appearance and can be used in salads or cooked, but it has a stronger, more assertive flavor. It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when the stalks are larger and slightly tougher. Chard is a popular ornamental vegetable that adds color to the vegetable garden.\"]", "time_sec": 37.078, "generation_time_seconds": 37.0748, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "record_process