{"id": "246", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between the Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA) and the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) are:\n\n1. Scope of application: The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.\n2. Definition of disability: The FEHA requires that a disability must limit a major life activity, while the ADA requires that a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.\n3. Number of employees: The ADA applies to employers with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA has no such requirement.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between FEHA and ADA is the scope of application and the definition of disability.", "pred": "The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, while FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, for FEHA, a disability is required, whereas under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, while FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, for FEHA, a disability is required, whereas under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.", "cot": "[\"The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, while FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, for FEHA, a disability is required, whereas under the ADA, a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.\", \"The difference between FEHA and ADA is that FEHA applies to more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers, while the ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees. Additionally, FEHA requires only that a mental and physical disability limit a major life activity, whereas the ADA requires a substantial limit to a major life activity.\", \"The difference between the FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, whereas the FEHA affects more employees, and it doesn't distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.\", \"Step 1: Identify the key differences between FEHA and ADA mentioned in the passages.\\n\\nStep 2: Summarize the differences:\\n- ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA affects more employees.\\n- FEHA does not distinguish between private and public employers, whereas ADA does.\\n- One of the most important differences is that FEHA requires only a mental and physical disability to limit a major life activity, while ADA requires a physical or mental impairment to substantially limit a major life activity.\\n\\nAnswer: The key differences between FEHA and ADA are that FEHA applies to more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers, while ADA applies to a specific number of employees in the private sector and distinguishes between private and public employers. Additionally, FEHA requires only a mental and physical disability to limit a major life activity, while ADA requires a physical or mental impairment to substantially limit a major life activity.\"]", "time_sec": 15.827, "generation_time_seconds": 15.819, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [785, 78325, 16790, 311, 678, 22426, 304, 279, 869, 10