o not lose weight when you have a bowel movement. The passages discuss that your caloric intake and expenditure are tied to healthy weight loss, and that a decrease in body weight after a bowel movement does not come from a loss of body fat in stools. Instead, body fat loss comes from creating a calorie deficit, which forces your body to burn stored fat for energy. Therefore, a bowel movement does not contribute to weight loss."}], "input_len": 498, "output_tokens": 394, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.24, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "357", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "gt": "An ad hominem fallacy occurs when someone attacks the character, motive, or other personal attribute of the person making an argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. This is often done to invalidate their arguments by associating their character with the soundness of their argument. On the other hand, the poisoning the well fallacy, which is a type of ad hominem attack, occurs when a person's integrity or intelligence is attacked before the merits of a case can be considered, effectively redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "pred": "The poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties, rather than addressing the actual issue at hand.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument to invalidate it, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is equating someone's character with the soundness of their argument, which is a logical fallacy.\n\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties, rather than addressing the actual issue at hand.", "cot": "[\"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument to invalidate it, rather than attacking the argument directly. An example of this is equating someone's character with the soundness of their argument, which is a logical fallacy.\\n\\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack on a person's integrity or intelligence that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered. It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties, rather than addressing the actual issue at hand.\", \"To answer the question, let's break down the information from the passages:\\n\\n1. Ad hominem fallacy:\\n   - It is an argument that attacks the person making the argument rather than addressing the argument itself.\\n   - It involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument.\\n   - An example of an ad hominem fallacy is when someone dismisses another person's argument by saying that the person is not a good person.\\n\\n2. Poisoning the well fallacy:\\n   - It is a type of ad hominem attack that involves attacking a person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of the argument can be considered.\\n   - It redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties, rather than focusing on the actual argument.\\n   - An example of the poisoning the well fallacy is when someone says that another person's argument is invalid because the person has a history of making false claims, without actually addressing the argument itself.\\n\\nThe difference between the two is that the ad hominem fallacy directly attacks the person making the argument, while the poisoning the well fallacy attacks the person's integrity or intelligence before the argument can be considered.\", \"Ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. Poisoning the well fallacy involves attacking the person's integrity or intelligence before the merits of a case can be considered, redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\", \"The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of the person making the argument to invalidate their argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. The poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, involves attacking the integrity or intelligence of one of the parties before discussing the merits of their arguments, thus redirecting the discussion away from the actual merits. The key difference lies in the timing and method of the attack: ad hominem occurs during or after the argument is presented, while poisoning the well happens beforehand.\"]", "time_sec": 22.3, "generation_time_seconds": 22.2965, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [785, 993, 4998, 68413, 4399, 2757, 17601, 22818, 279, 3668, 11, 45994, 11, 476, 1008, 8201, 315, 279, 1697, 3259, 279, 5693, 311, 39943, 432, 11, 4751, 1091, 22818, 279, 5693, 5961, 13, 1527, 3110, 315, 419, 374, 3232, 1095, 4325, 594, 3668, 448, 279, 5112, 2090, 315, 862, 5693, 11, 892, 374, 264, 19819, 4399, 2757, 382, 785, 51136, 279, 1632, 4399, 2757, 374, 458, 993, 4998, 68413, 3359, 389, 264, 1697, 594, 16614, 476, 11229, 429, 13666, 1573, 279, 49832, 315, 264, 1142, 646, 387, 6509, 13, 1084, 71171, 264, 10219, 311, 279, 56690, 315, 825, 315, 279, 9677, 11, 4751, 1091, 27020, 279, 5042, 4265, 518, 1424, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.5193, -0.0367, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0001, 0.0, -0.0939, -0.0414, -0.0234, -0.0623, -0.0363, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0029, -0.3024, -0.0, -0.0822, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.5065, -0.0, -3.8927, -0.0238, -2.5202, -0.2802, -0.0755, 0.0, -2.6102, -0.0, -0.0002, -1.0539, -0.006, -2.1095, -0.0646, -0.2649, -0.3144, -0.4092, -1.7517, 0.0, -0.0016, -0.0024, -0.0, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.6369, -0.0009, -0.0001, -0.0258, -0.0033, -0.0, 0.0, -0.3032, -0.0806, -0.0008, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, 0.0, -0.4042, -0.1093, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.2576, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.3609, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0438, -1.703, -0.0001, -0.0039, -0.0016, -0.0, -0.0242, -0.0, -0.0005, -0.0, -0.0034, -0.0963, -0.3614, -0.2146, -0.1789, -0.0, -0.0037, -0.0045, -0.0004, -0.