ollout": 3, "token_ids": [785, 21085, 1558, 537, 3410, 3151, 17452, 315, 279, 34048, 11174, 50655, 6422, 13, 1084, 1172, 33845, 429, 5257, 4494, 315, 25322, 323, 29949, 3063, 389, 279, 10779, 323, 304, 279, 12408, 11, 323, 5707, 458, 3110, 448, 67342, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.9956, -0.8224, -0.0956, -0.0, -0.0565, -0.0124, -0.5112, -0.0016, -0.0011, -0.0019, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0364, -0.864, -0.1273, -0.0039, -0.0408, -0.7101, -0.0083, 0.0, -0.0045, -0.0125, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0018, -0.0036, -0.0019, -0.0002, -0.0024, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1245, -0.1409, -4.2302, -0.2545, -0.0, -3.9803, -0.0003, -1.1967, -3.3188], "cumulative_logprob": -17.5623, "norm_logp": -0.4283, "input_len": 500, "output_tokens": 41, "pred": "The passage does not provide specific characteristics of the tropical rainforest floor. It only mentions that various types of fruits and nuts grow on the plants and in the trees, and provides an example with bananas."}], "input_len": 500, "output_tokens": 604, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 9.41, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "521", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how raffles work:\n\nA raffle is a game of chance where participants buy tickets with a unique number, and a prize is awarded to the holder of the winning ticket drawn at random. In a reverse raffle, the process is similar, but the tickets are numbered from 1 to approximately 55, and tables consist of 20 individuals, constituting one complete ticket at $1000, with two $500 prizes available. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and the gross proceeds go into a prize pool, with 50% going to the winner of the random draw and the remaining 50% going to a charitable cause.\n\nTherefore, raffles typically involve buying tickets with unique numbers, and a random draw determines the winner, with a percentage of the proceeds often going to a charitable cause.", "pred": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants. A random drawing is conducted to determine the winner(s). The proceeds from the ticket sales are used to fund the prize(s).", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To answer the question \"how do raffles work,\" let's break down the information provided by the passages:\n\n1. **Reverse Raffle (Passage 1)**:\n   - It is described as a \"raffle but backwards.\"\n   - Entry tickets have a number from 1 to approximately 55.\n   - Tickets cost $50 each.\n   - A table consists of 20 individuals, making one complete ticket at $1000.\n   - There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\n\n2. **50-50 Draw (Passage 2)**:\n   - It is described as a raffle held during a single day at a particular event.\n   - Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the stadium during the event by roaming staff.\n   - The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold go into a prize pool.\n   - 50% of the prize pool goes to the winner of the random draw.\n   - The remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose.\n\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffle (Passage 3)**:\n   - Not included in the ticket price.\n   - Opportunity to win $10,000.\n   - This suggests additional raffles or special draws within the event.\n\nBased on these passages, the general workings of raffles include:\n- **Ticket Sales**: Tickets are sold to participants.\n- **Drawing**: A random drawing is conducted to determine the winner(s).\n- **Prize Distribution**: The proceeds from the ticket sales are used to fund the prize(s).\n\nAnswer: Raffles work by selling tickets to participants. A random drawing is conducted to determine the winner(s). The proceeds from the ticket sales are used to fund the prize(s).", "cot": "[\"To answer the question \\\"how do raffles work,\\\" let's break down the information provided by the passages:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle (Passage 1)**:\\n   - It is described as a \\\"raffle but backwards.\\\"\\n   - Entry tickets have a number from 1 to approximately 55.\\n   - Tickets cost $50 each.\\n   - A table consists of 20 individuals, making one complete ticket at $1000.\\n   - There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw (Passage 2)**:\\n   - It is described as a raffle held during a single day at a particular event.\\n   - Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the stadium during the event by roaming staff.\\n   - The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold go into a prize pool.\\n   - 50% of the prize pool goes to the winner of the random draw.\\n   - The remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffle (Passage 3)**:\\n   - Not included in the ticket price.\\n   - Opportunity to win $10,000.\\n   - This suggests additional raffles or special draws within the event.\\n\\nBased on these passages, the general workings of raffles include:\\n- **Ticket Sales**: Tickets are sold to participants.\\n- **Drawing**: A random drawing is conducted to determine the winner(s).\\n- **Prize Distribution**: The proceeds from the ticket sales are used to fund the prize(s).\\n\\nAnswer: Raffles work by selling tickets to participants. A random drawing is conducted to determine the winner(s). The proceeds from the ticket sales are used to fund the prize(s).\", \"The passage describes two types of raffles: a reverse raffle and a 50-50 draw. Here's how they work:\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle**:\\n   - Each entry ticket has a number from 1 to approximately 55.\\n   - Tickets cost $50 each.\\n   - A table consists of 20 individuals, which constitutes one complete ticket at $1000.\\n   - Two $500 prizes are awarded: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw**:\\n   - Tickets are sold to patrons during a single-day event.\\n   - The gross proceeds of the tickets go into a prize pool.\\n   - 50% of the prize pool goes to the winner of the random draw at the end of the event.\\n   - The remaining 50% is set aside for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\nIn summary, raffles typically involve selling tickets for a chance to win a prize. The reverse raffle has a set of rules for drawing tickets and awarding prizes, while the 50-50 draw involves a random draw with a split of the proceeds between the winner and a charitable cause.\\n\\nAnswer: Raffles work by selling tickets for a chance to win a prize. The reverse raffle has a set of rules for drawing tickets and awarding prizes, while the 50-50 draw involves a random draw with a split of the proceeds between the winner and a charitable cause.\", \"Raffles typically involve selling tickets to a large number of participants, with the chance of winning prizes. The winner is usually determined by a random drawing of the tickets. The prize money is often predetermined and can range from small cash amounts to larger sums, such as $10,000 as mentioned in the passage. The cost of the tickets can vary, with examples given in the passage being $50 or $1000 for a complete ticket.\", \"Based on the provided passages, a reverse raffle works as follows: Your entry ticket has a number on it from 1 to approximately 55. Tickets are $50 each, and a table consists of 20 individuals, which constitutes one complete ticket at $1000. Additionally, there are two $500 prizes (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled). Individuals totaling twenty.\"]", "time_sec": 36.728, "generation_time_seconds": 36.7202, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 4226, 279, 3405, 330, 5158, 653, 435, 80535, 975, 1335, 1077, 594, 1438, 1495, 279, 1995, 3897, 553, 279, 46769, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 45695, 431, 44090, 320, 12187, 424, 220, 16, 32295, 510, 256, 481, 1084, 374, 7481, 438, 264, 330, 956, 542, 273, 714, 28412, 10040, 256, 481, 15788, 14403, 614, 264, 1372, 504, 220, 16, 311, 13187, 220, 20, 20, 624, 256, 481, 35615, 2783, 400, 20, 15, 1817, 624, 256, 481, 362, 1965, 17167, 315, 220, 17, 15, 7775, 11, 3259, 825, 4583, 11727, 518, 400, 16, 15, 15, 15, 624, 256, 481, 2619, 525, 1378, 400, 20, 15, 15, 37408, 25, 825, 369, 279, 4124, 11958, 13330, 323, 825, 369, 279, 1156, 11727, 13238, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 11992, 320, 12187, 424, 220, 17, 32295, 510, 256, 481, 1084, 374, 7481, 438, 264, 435, 44090, 5644, 2337, 264, 3175, 1899, 518, 264, 3953, 1538, 624, 256, 481, 35615, 525, 6088, 311, 51760, 6814, 279, 23889, 2337, 279, 1538, 553, 68253, 5570, 624, 256, 481, 576, 19952, 33455, 315, 220, 20, 15, 12, 20, 15, 14403, 6088, 728, 1119, 264, 21882, 7314, 624, 256, 481, 220, 20, 15, 4, 315, 279, 21882, 7314, 5780, 311, 279, 13632, 315, 279, 4194, 4038, 624, 256, 481, 576, 9664, 220, 20, 15, 4, 374, 738, 15663, 369, 264, 47701, 7428, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 47441, 29873, 8751, 431, 44090, 320, 12187, 424, 220, 18, 32295, 510, 256, 481, 2806, 5230, 304, 279, 11727, 3349, 624, 256, 481, 47279, 311, 3164, 400, 16, 15, 11, 15, 15,