, 1896, 975, 700, 389, 279, 5636, 13, 576, 5163, 4573, 315, 379, 477, 722, 682, 374, 902, 5021, 9760, 11, 323, 702, 1012, 4429, 916, 553, 11048, 29016, 49825, 11, 6388, 311, 7172, 15024, 323, 25148, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.3828, -0.0, -3.3442, -0.0043, -0.0002, -0.023, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0013, -0.0, -0.0, -0.861, -0.2572, -0.0127, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.0, 0.0, -0.3084, -0.4116, -0.0017, -0.0002, -0.0154, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0118, -2.3874, -0.0687, -0.0, -0.0191, -0.0234, -0.0, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0528, -0.0036, -0.0, -0.3135, -0.2653, -0.0865, -0.2317, -0.0, -1.3496, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0004, -1.3528, -0.3674, 0.0, -0.1126, -0.0189, -0.0036, -0.0023, -0.2112, -0.0248], "cumulative_logprob": -13.5319, "norm_logp": -0.2255, "input_len": 327, "output_tokens": 60, "pred": "Technology has drastically changed the attitude and processes of the workplace, allowing employees to interact with a global market and even take work out on the road. The office environment of yesteryear is no longer relevant, and has been taken over by rapid technological advancement, leading to increased efficiency and productivity."}], "input_len": 327, "output_tokens": 540, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.25, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "633", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "The nervous system and endocrine system have both similarities and differences. \n\nSimilarities:\n1. Both systems are dedicated to maintaining homeostasis and coordinating the activities of cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\n3. Chemical messengers play a crucial role in both systems.\n\nDifferences:\n1. The nervous system is composed of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system occurs through electrochemical pulses, while chemical hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the endocrine system.\n3. The nervous system utilizes interconnected cells to transmit signals, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system transmits signals through neurons, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.", "pred": "[Similarities: Both systems maintain homeostasis through coordination and regulation, regulated by negative feedback mechanisms, and both use chemical messengers. Differences: Neurotransmission vs Hormonal transmission, continuous vs discrete structure, neurons vs glands, neural vs circulatory signal transmission.]", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system include:\n1. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\n\nDifferences between the nervous system and the endocrine system include:\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas the Chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System.\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, while the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, whereas the circulatory system is used by the Endocrine System to transmit signals.\n\nAnswer:[Similarities: Both systems maintain homeostasis through coordination and regulation, regulated by negative feedback mechanisms, and both use chemical messengers. Differences: Neurotransmission vs Hormonal transmission, continuous vs discrete structure, neurons vs glands, neural vs circulatory signal transmission.]", "cot": "[\"Similarities between the nervous system and the endocrine system include:\\n1. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences between the nervous system and the endocrine system include:\\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas the Chemicals known as hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, while the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, whereas the circulatory system is used by the Endocrine System to transmit signals.\\n\\nAnswer:[Similarities: Both systems maintain homeostasis through coordination and regulation, regulated by negative feedback mechanisms, and both use chemical messengers. Differences: Neurotransmission vs Hormonal transmission, continuous vs discrete structure, neurons vs glands, neural vs circulatory signal transmission.]\", \"Similarities:\\n- Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis.\\n- Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n- Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n- The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the Endocrine System is made of glands.\\n- Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, while in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones.\\n- The nervous system is continuous with its cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\\n- The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"Similarities:\\n1. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n2. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems, although their method of transmission and release differs in the two systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n1. The nervous system is made of neuron cells while the Endocrine System is made of glands.\\n2. Transmission is performed by the electrochemical pulses in the nervous system, whereas hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the Endocrine System.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, while the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit the signal, and the circulatory system is used by the Endocrine System to transmit the signals.\", \"Similarities:\\nBoth the nervous system and the endocrine system are involved in maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems. Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n1. Composition: The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\\n2. Transmission: The nervous system transmits signals through electrochemical pulses, whereas the endocrine system uses hormones, which are chemicals, for signal transmission.\\n3. Physical connection: The nervous system is continuous, with cells interconnected, whereas the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. Signal transmission methods: The nervous system utilizes neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\\n\\nAnswer:[The nervous system is made of neuron cells, transmits signals through electrochemical pulses, is continuous, and utilizes neurons to transmit signals. The endocrine system is made of glands, transmits signals through hormones, is discrete, and uses the circulatory system to transmit signals. Both systems maintain homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems, and are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.]\"]", "time_sec": 34.26, "generation_time_seconds": 34.2552, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0007, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [34402, 1361, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 2924, 510, 16, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 28229, 311, 20337, 2114, 535, 10718, 553, 65415, 323, 57399, 279, 7488, 315, 1008, 7761, 11, 38781, 11, 35753, 11, 323, 5942, 624, 17, 13, 11733, 5942, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 624, 18, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 2989, 304, 2176, 5942, 382, 35, 58326, 1948, 279, 22596, 1849, 323, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 2924, 510, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 1865, 315, 48284, 7761, 11, 1393, 279, 3972, 77638, 73