{"id": "430", "question": "how do twins happen", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how twins happen:\n\n* Monoamniotic twins: These twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization.\n* Conjoined twins: When the division of the developing zygote into 2 embryos occurs, it happens within 8 days of fertilization, and mortality is highest for conjoined twins due to the many complications resulting from shared organs.\n* Dizygotic twin pregnancies: These twins are slightly more likely to occur in women who are of West African descent (especially Yoruba), between the ages of 30 and 40, are taller and heavier than average, and have had several previous pregnancies.\n* Identical twins (monozygotic): These twins develop from one zygote, which splits and forms two embryos.\n* Fraternal twins (dizygotic): These twins develop from two different eggs, and each twin is fertilized by its own sperm cell.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages: The passage does not provide information on the exact timing or mechanism of twinning beyond what is mentioned above.", "pred": "Twins occur due to the development of two offspring from the same pregnancy. They can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning that they develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning that they develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell. Monozygotic twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs. Factors that increase the likelihood of dizygotic twins include being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), being between the age of 30 and 40 years, being greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies. The general term for one offspring of a multiple birth is multiple.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the types of twins mentioned in the passages.\n- Monozygotic twins: These develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos.\n- Dizygotic twins: These develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm.\n\nStep 2: Understand the timing of the split in monozygotic twins.\n- Monozygotic twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization.\n\nStep 3: Understand the timing of the split in dizygotic twins.\n- Dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, meaning each egg is fertilized separately.\n\nStep 4: Consider the factors that increase the likelihood of dizygotic twins.\n- Factors that increase the likelihood include being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), being between the age of 30 and 40 years, being greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies.\n\nStep 5: Understand the general term for one offspring of a multiple birth.\n- The general term for one offspring of a multiple birth is multiple.\n\nAnswer: Twins occur due to the development of two offspring from the same pregnancy. They can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning that they develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning that they develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell. Monozygotic twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs. Factors that increase the likelihood of dizygotic twins include being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), being between the age of 30 and 40 years, being greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies. The general term for one offspring of a multiple birth is multiple.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the types of twins mentioned in the passages.\\n- Monozygotic twins: These develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos.\\n- Dizygotic twins: These develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm.\\n\\nStep 2: Understand the timing of the split in monozygotic twins.\\n- Monozygotic twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization.\\n\\nStep 3: Understand the timing of the split in dizygotic twins.\\n- Dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs, meaning each egg is fertilized separately.\\n\\nStep 4: Consider the factors that increase the likelihood of dizygotic twins.\\n- Factors that increase the likelihood include being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), being between the age of 30 and 40 years, being greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies.\\n\\nStep 5: Understand the general term for one offspring of a multiple birth.\\n- The general term for one offspring of a multiple birth is multiple.\\n\\nAnswer: Twins occur due to the development of two offspring from the same pregnancy. They can be either monozygotic (identical), meaning that they develop from one zygote that splits and forms two embryos, or dizygotic (fraternal), meaning that they develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell. Monozygotic twins occur when the split takes place after the ninth day after fertilization, while dizygotic twins develop from two different eggs. Factors that increase the likelihood of dizygotic twins include being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), being between the age of 30 and 40 years, being greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies. The general term for one offspring of a multiple birth is multiple.\", \"Twins can happen in two main ways, depending on the type of zygote that splits and forms embryos. \\n\\n1. Monozygotic twins, also known as identical twins, result from the division of a single fertilized egg (zygote) into two embryos. If this division occurs within 8 days after fertilization, the resulting twins will be conjoined twins, sharing many organs. If the division occurs after the ninth day, the twins will be monoamniotic twins, sharing the same amniotic sac and potentially the same placenta.\\n\\n2. Dizygotic twins, also known as fraternal twins, develop from two separate eggs, each fertilized by a different sperm cell. This type of twinning is less common but more likely to occur under certain conditions, such as the woman being of West African descent, between 30 and 40 years of age, greater than average height and weight, or having had several previous pregnancies.\\n\\nAnswer:[Twins can happen in two main ways: monozygotic twins, which develop from a single fertilized egg that splits into two embryos, and dizygotic twins, which develop from two separate eggs, each fertilized by a different sperm cell.]\", \"Twins can occur in two ways: monozygotic (identical) or dizygotic (fraternal). In monozygotic twins, the zygote splits and forms two embryos, while in dizygotic twins, each twin is fertilized by its own sperm cell.\", \"Twins can happen in several ways. Monozygotic twins, also known as identical twins, occur when the division of the developing zygote into two embryos happens within 8 days of fertilization. This division takes place after the ninth day in the case of monoamniotic twins. In contrast, dizygotic twins, or fraternal twins, develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell. Factors that slightly increase the likelihood of dizygotic twin pregnancies include being of West African descent (especially Yoruba), being between the age of 30 and 40 years, being greater than average height and weight, and having had several previous pregnancies.\\n\\nAnswer: Twins can occur in two main ways: monozygotic (identical) twins, which result from the division of the developing zygote into two embryos within 8 days of fertilization, and dizygotic (fraternal) twins, which develop from two different eggs, each fertilized by its own sperm cell.\"]", "ti