{"id": "95", "question": "what is the difference between essential and nonessential amino acids", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nEssential amino acids are those that cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids are those that can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\n\nPassage 1 states that nine amino acids are essential for humans, but only eight of them are essential for adults (histidine is only essential for infants). Passage 2 explains that nonessential amino acids are not unnecessary, as the body can produce them from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. Passage 3 reiterates that nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, while essential amino acids cannot be produced and must be obtained from the diet.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between essential and nonessential amino acids is that essential amino acids cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down protein products.", "pred": "Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Determine the key information about essential and nonessential amino acids from the passages.\n\nFrom passage 1:\n- Nine out of the 20 amino acids are essential.\n- Adults only need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan.\n- The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants.\n\nFrom passage 2:\n- Nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.\n- Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet.\n\nFrom passage 3:\n- Amino acids are the building blocks of proteins.\n- Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet.\n- Nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.\n\nStep 2: Compare essential and nonessential amino acids based on the information provided.\n\nEssential amino acids:\n- Cannot be made by the body.\n- Must be obtained from the diet.\n- There are nine essential amino acids for adults (eight for infants), including valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan.\n\nNonessential amino acids:\n- Can be made by the body.\n- Do not need to be obtained from the diet.\n- There are eleven nonessential amino acids, including alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, and tyrosine.\n\nStep 3: Formulate the answer based on the comparison.\n\nAnswer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Determine the key information about essential and nonessential amino acids from the passages.\\n\\nFrom passage 1:\\n- Nine out of the 20 amino acids are essential.\\n- Adults only need to obtain eight essential amino acids: valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan.\\n- The ninth essential amino acid, histidine, is only essential for infants.\\n\\nFrom passage 2:\\n- Nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.\\n- Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet.\\n\\nFrom passage 3:\\n- Amino acids are the building blocks of proteins.\\n- Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body, so they must be obtained from the diet.\\n- Nonessential amino acids can be made by the body.\\n\\nStep 2: Compare essential and nonessential amino acids based on the information provided.\\n\\nEssential amino acids:\\n- Cannot be made by the body.\\n- Must be obtained from the diet.\\n- There are nine essential amino acids for adults (eight for infants), including valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan.\\n\\nNonessential amino acids:\\n- Can be made by the body.\\n- Do not need to be obtained from the diet.\\n- There are eleven nonessential amino acids, including alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, and tyrosine.\\n\\nStep 3: Formulate the answer based on the comparison.\\n\\nAnswer: Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body and do not need to be obtained from the diet.\", \"Essential amino acids are those that cannot be produced by the body and must be obtained from the diet. Nonessential amino acids, on the other hand, can be synthesized by the body from other amino acids or by breaking down proteins. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine. Essential amino acids are necessary for the body to build proteins, which are crucial for cell repair, growth, and maintenance.\", \"Step 1: Identify the definition of essential amino acids given in the passages.\\n- Essential amino acids are those that adults must obtain from their diet because they cannot be made by the body. These include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan.\\n\\nStep 2: Identify the definition of nonessential amino acids given in the passages.\\n- Nonessential amino acids are those that can be made by the body, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products. They include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\\n\\nStep 3: Compare the two types of amino acids.\\n- Essential amino acids are crucial for adults' health as they cannot be produced by the body, whereas nonessential amino acids can be synthesized within the body or obtained from dietary sources.\\n\\nStep 4: Formulate the answer based on the information provided.\\n- The difference between essential and nonessential amino acids lies in their ability to be produced by the body. Essential amino acids must be obtained through diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body or obtained from dietary sources.\\n\\nAnswer: Essential amino acids are those that adults must obtain from their diet because they cannot be made by the body, while nonessential amino acids can be made by the body, either from essential amino acids or by breaking down protein products.\", \"Essential amino acids cannot be made by the body and must be obtained from the diet, while nonessential amino acids can be produced by the body and are not necessary to obtain through diet. Essential amino acids include valine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, and tryptophan. Nonessential amino acids include alanine, asparagine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, arginine, cysteine, glutamine, glycine, ornithine, proline, serine, and tyrosine.\"]", "time_sec": 33.7, "generation_time_seconds": 33.6946, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 29901, 279, 1376, 1995, 911, 7565, 323, 2477, 48482, 41400, 32869, 504, 279, 46769, 382, 3830, 21085, 220, 16, 510, 12, 37066, 700, 315, 279, 220, 17, 15, 41400, 32869, 525, 7565, 624, 12, 57238, 1172, 1184, 311, 6851, 8063, 7565, 41400, 32869, 25, 1044, 482, 11, 374, 1263, 1754