{"id": "124", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 70 2/4\" and set and level them according to the shed building plans. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4\" and six joists at 44 3/4\". Assemble the floor and place it on the skids as indicated in the floor framing section of the plans. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles. Cut two more 4 x 4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids and, when the frame is square, toenail the floor joists to the skids. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The walls for the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. The size of the shed can be adjusted as needed.", "pred": "For the walls, construct them separately and then raise them onto the frame. The walls are built using cut pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids. Position the frame on the skids, toenail the floor joists to the skids, and then attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The shed dimensions provided are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but you can easily expand these dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To build a lean-to shed, first prepare the site by laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Next, cut two 4x4 skids to a length of 70 2/4 inches and set them up on the gravel, ensuring they are level. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to a length of 7 3/4 inches and six joists to a length of 44 3/4 inches. Assemble the floor and place it on the skids, checking for square, and then secure the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.\n\nFor the walls, construct them separately and then raise them onto the frame. The walls are built using cut pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids. Position the frame on the skids, toenail the floor joists to the skids, and then attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The shed dimensions provided are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but you can easily expand these dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.", "cot": "[\"To build a lean-to shed, first prepare the site by laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Next, cut two 4x4 skids to a length of 70 2/4 inches and set them up on the gravel, ensuring they are level. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to a length of 7 3/4 inches and six joists to a length of 44 3/4 inches. Assemble the floor and place it on the skids, checking for square, and then secure the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.\\n\\nFor the walls, construct them separately and then raise them onto the frame. The walls are built using cut pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids. Position the frame on the skids, toenail the floor joists to the skids, and then attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The shed dimensions provided are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but you can easily expand these dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\", \"Answer: Building a lean-to shed requires several steps, including preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel and cutting two 4 x 4 skids to 70 2/4 inches. The skids should be set and leveled according to the shed building plans. Two 2 x 6 rim joists should also be cut at 7 3/4 inches, and six joists at 44 3/4 inches. The floor should be assembled and set on the skids, checking for square, and then anchored to the skids with four joist clip angles.\", \"Building a lean-to shed involves several steps, starting with preparing the site. First, you should prepare the site by laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Next, measure and cut the two 4 x 4 skids to a specified length, which is 70 2/4 inches, and set them at a level position following the shed building plans. After that, cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4 inches and six joists at 44 3/4 inches. Assemble the floor and place it on the skids, ensuring it is square. Then, anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. The walls of the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. For the floor framing, cut two pressure-treated 2 by 4s to a length of 6 feet for the rim joists.\", \"To build a lean-to shed, follow these steps based on the provided passages:\\n\\n1. **Prepare the Site**: Lay a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel on the site to provide a stable foundation.\\n\\n2. **Cut and Measure 4x4 Skids**: Measure and cut two 4x4 skids to the required length (70 2/4 inches). These skids will serve as the base for the shed.\\n\\n3. **Level the Skids**: Set the skids on the prepared gravel layer and level them. This ensures that the shed will be stable and level.\\n\\n4. **Cut Rim Joists**: Cut two 2x6 rim joists to a length of 7 3/4 inches. Additionally, cut six more joists to a length of 44 3/4 inches.\\n\\n5. **Assemble the Floor**: Use the rim joists and joists to assemble the floor. Place the assembled floor on the skids. Check the squareness of the frame and secure it to the skids with four joist clip angles.\\n\\n6. **Install the Floor Joists**: Position the frame on the skids and toenail the floor joists to the skids when they are square.\\n\\n7. **Attach Flooring**: Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame.\\n\\n8. **Construct the Walls**: The walls for the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame.\\n\\nFor the shed dimensions:\\n- The shed shown is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.\\n- You can easily expand the dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n\\nFollowing these steps will help you build a sturdy and well-constructed lean-to shed.\"]", "time_sec": 29.757, "generation_time_seconds": 29.7535, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0006, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 1936, 