{"id": "431", "question": "how to clean tarnished jewelry at home", "gt": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, first determine the extent of the tarnish. If the jewelry is slightly dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip a soft cloth into the solution. Rub the jewelry with the cloth, rinse in cool water, and buff with a cloth until dry. If the tarnish is heavier, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the jewelry and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth. If your silver jewelry is lightly tarnished, you can also try submerging it in a hot saltwater bath. Ensure that if your jewelry has gemstones, they won't be affected by the saltwater. For diamonds, clean with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, and pat dry with a soft cloth.", "pred": "These methods are effective for cleaning tarnished jewelry at home, and you can choose the one that best suits your needs and the type of jewelry you are cleaning.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\n\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).\n\n3. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. Make sure to check if your jewelry has gemstones that won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\n\nThese methods are effective for cleaning tarnished jewelry at home, and you can choose the one that best suits your needs and the type of jewelry you are cleaning.", "cot": "[\"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can follow these steps:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\\n\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).\\n\\n3. Alternatively, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. You just need enough to cover the jewelry you're cleaning. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. Make sure to check if your jewelry has gemstones that won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\\n\\nThese methods are effective for cleaning tarnished jewelry at home, and you can choose the one that best suits your needs and the type of jewelry you are cleaning.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use different methods depending on the severity of the tarnish. For items that are merely dull, filmy, or starting to discolor, you can mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, rinse it in cool water, and buff it with a soft cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, you can make a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it to the jewelry with a soft, lint-free cloth. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. For more severe tarnish, you can use a different solution or method, such as using warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush, as suggested by Loretta Castoro, vice president and international jewelry buyer for Jewelry Television, and then pat it dry with a soft cloth.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can follow these methods:\\n\\n1. For items that are merely dull, filmy or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry.\\n2. For heavier tarnish, mix a paste of three parts baking soda to one part water. Wet the silver and apply the cleaner with a soft, lint-free cloth (not paper towels, which can scratch).\\n3. For diamonds, the best way to clean is with warm soapy water and a soft-bristle brush or old toothbrush. Pat it dry with a soft cloth.\\n4. For silver jewelry, pour two cups of hot water into a bowl. This method works as a gentle cleanser that removes tarnish without abrading the silver. If your silver is lightly tarnished, the salt bath should take the tarnish right off. If you're cleaning a lot of jewelry at once, you can use more water. For just one piece of jewelry, use less water. If your jewelry has gemstones, make sure they won't be affected by being submerged in a saltwater solution.\", \"To clean tarnished jewelry at home, you can use a variety of methods depending on the severity of the tarnish. For items that are merely dull or starting to discolor, mix a few drops of mild dish soap with warm water and dip the jewelry in a soft cloth. Rub the jewelry, then rinse it in cool water and buff with a cloth until dry. For heavier tarnish, make a paste using three parts baking soda to one part water and apply it to the silver with a soft, lint-free cloth. It's important to avoid using paper towels as they can scratch the silver.\"]", "time_sec": 34.902, "generation_time_seconds": 34.8959, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0007, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 4240, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 498, 646, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1752, 3589, 429, 525, 16234, 40530, 11, 89394, 11, 476, 5916, 311, 2560, 789, 11, 6514, 264, 2421, 21025, 315, 23034, 12000, 26785, 448, 8205, 3015, 323, 23604, 304, 264, 8413, 27292, 13, 12839, 279, 30717, 11, 1221, 73241, 304, 7010, 3015, 323, 11522, 448, 264, 27292, 3080, 9058, 382, 17, 13, 1752, 43822, 86480, 812, 11, 6514, 264, 24937, 315, 2326, 5479, 27815, 38862, 311, 825, 949, 3015, 13, 44856, 279, 14961, 323, 3796, 279, 31881, 448, 264, 8413, 11, 57920, 12577, 27292, 320, 1921, 5567, 52884, 11, 892, 646, 18778, 3593, 18, 13, 38478, 11, 4914, 1378, 25374, 315, 4017, 3015, 1119, 264, 19212, 13, 1446, 1101, 1184, 3322, 311, 3421, 279, 30717, 498, 2299, 15826, 13, 1096, 1714, 4278, 438, 264, 21700, 34194, 261, 429, 28160, 86480, 812, 2041, 668, 81, 2228, 279, 14961, 13, 1416, 697, 14961, 374, 33404, 86480, 3304, 11, 279, 12021, 8885, 1265, 1896, 279, 86480, 812, 1290, 1007, 13, 1416, 498, 2299, 15826, 264, 2696, 315, 30717, 518, 3055, 11, 990, 803, 3015, 13, 1752, 1101, 825, 6573, 315, 30717, 11, 990, 2686, 3015, 13, 7405, 2704, 311, 1779, 421, 697, 30717, 702, 18747, 32510, 429, 2765, 944, 387, 11495, 553, 1660, 84077, 304, 264, 12021, 12987, 6291, 382, 9485, 5413, 525, 7373, 369, 15826, 86480, 3304, 30717, 518, 2114, 11, 323, 498, 646, 5157, 279, 825, 429, 1850, 27976, 697, 3880, 323, 279, 943, 315, 30717, 498, 525, 15826, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1456, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0002, 0.0, -0.0014, -0.2326, -0.0008, -1.1975, -0.1883, -0.5083, -0.3803, -0.0033, -0.0, -1.1376, -0.0256, -0.0071, -0.0004, -0.0014, -0.0, -0.0647, -0.0001, -0.1174, -0.0, -0.0001, 0.0, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0386, -0.0129, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, 0.