rly tall, consider using a wedge technique. This involves positioning yourself between the basket and the offensive player, creating a barrier that the tall player must go around to get to the basket.\n4. Take advantage of your height or position relative to the tall player. For example, if you are a 6'6 guard and the tall player is 7'2, you can use your height to your advantage by staying close to them and limiting their shooting range.\n5. Be flexible and adapt to the situation. If the tall player is particularly tall and difficult to guard, consider using a different strategy or adjusting your approach based on the specific player and game situation.\nBy following these steps, you can effectively guard a tall person in basketball, even if they are larger or taller than you."}, {"rollout": 3, "token_ids": [1249, 7616, 264, 16217, 1697, 304, 19240, 11, 498, 1265, 4717, 438, 3265, 438, 3204, 311, 1105, 13, 1096, 646, 387, 16994, 553, 22040, 33832, 697, 7541, 504, 3108, 311, 3108, 311, 4717, 3143, 697, 14704, 323, 29337, 862, 6552, 10488, 13, 22406, 11, 432, 594, 2989, 311, 728, 448, 279, 7411, 498, 525, 25934, 11, 7290, 498, 1184, 311, 10515, 311, 697, 2309, 323, 279, 2608, 315, 697, 14704, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.136, -0.0029, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0044, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0143, -0.5124, -0.0656, -0.2851, -0.0963, -0.0, -0.0008, -0.0, -0.0004, -1.0632, -3.3314, -2.513, -2.1298, -0.0449, -0.2579, -0.0001, -0.4382, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0435, -0.1595, -0.0001, -0.0047, -0.0, -0.0741, -0.0059, -0.5947, -0.0009, -0.0, -0.004, -0.2006, 0.0, -2.4712, -0.414, -0.0248, -0.0001, -3.0021, -0.0, -0.0093, -0.0074, -0.0, -0.005, -0.0, -0.2666, -0.8031, -0.078, -1.5007, 0.0, -0.5003, -0.2581, -1.8494, -2.9064, -0.0388, -0.4066, -0.4036, -0.0946, -0.5947, -0.0052, -0.007, -0.851], "cumulative_logprob": -28.4837, "norm_logp": -0.3956, "input_len": 323, "output_tokens": 72, "pred": "To guard a tall person in basketball, you should stay as close as possible to them. This can be achieved by actively sliding your feet from side to side to stay near your opponent and minimize their shot opportunities. Additionally, it's important to go with the cards you are dealt, meaning you need to adapt to your position and the height of your opponent."}], "input_len": 323, "output_tokens": 444, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.72, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 8.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "635", "question": "similarity and difference between nervous system and endocrine system", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are the key similarities and differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system:\n\nSimilarities:\n\n* Both systems are involved in maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\n* Both systems use chemical messengers (neurotransmitters and hormones) to transmit signals.\n* Both systems have negative feedback mechanisms to regulate their function.\n\nDifferences:\n\n* The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is composed of glands.\n* Transmission of signals occurs through electrical impulses in the nervous system, while hormones are responsible for signal transmission in the endocrine system.\n* The nervous system has a continuous structure, with cells connected to each other, while the endocrine system consists of discrete organs not physically connected to each other.\n* The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system to transmit signals.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "pred": "Differences:\n1. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones.\n3. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, while the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, whereas the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Similarities:\n1. Both systems maintain homeostasis.\n2. They are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\n\nDifferences:\n1. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones.\n3. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, while the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\n4. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, whereas the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission.", "cot": "[\"Similarities:\\n1. Both systems maintain homeostasis.\\n2. They are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n1. The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made up of glands.\\n2. Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by chemicals known as hormones.\\n3. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, while the organs of the endocrine system are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, whereas the endocrine system relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission.\", \"Similarities:\\n- Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis.\\n- Both systems are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n- Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n- The nervous system is made of neuron cells, while the endocrine system is made of glands.\\n- Transmission in the nervous system is performed by electrochemical pulses, whereas in the endocrine system, it is done by hormones.\\n- The nervous system is continuous with interconnected cells, whereas the endocrine organs are discrete and not physically connected.\\n- The nervous system uses neurons to transmit signals, while the endocrine system uses the circulatory system to transmit signals.\", \"To answer the question about the similarities and differences between the nervous system and the endocrine system, let's break down the information provided in the passages:\\n\\n### Similarities:\\n1. **Purpose of Regulation**: Both systems are involved in maintaining homeostasis by coordinating and regulating the activities of other cells, tissues, organs, and systems.\\n2. **Negative Feedback Mechanisms**: Both systems utilize negative feedback mechanisms to regulate their functions.\\n3. **Chemical Messengers**: Both systems use chemical messengers to communicate with target cells or organs.\\n\\n### Differences:\\n1. **Composition**:\\n   - **Nervous System**: Composed of neuron cells.\\n   - **Endocrine System**: Composed of glands.\\n2. **Transmission Mechanism**:\\n   - **Nervous System**: Uses electrochemical pulses for signal transmission.\\n   - **Endocrine System**: Relies on hormones (chemicals) for signal transmission.\\n3. **Physical Connection**:\\n   - **Nervous System**: Continuous with interconnected cells.\\n   - **Endocrine System**: Organs are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. **Signal Transmission**:\\n   - **Nervous System**: Utilizes the neurons for direct transmission.\\n   - **Endocrine System**: Uses the circulatory system to transmit signals indirectly.\\n\\n### Final Answer:\\nThe nervous system and the endocrine system have several similarities and differences. Both systems are crucial for maintaining homeostasis and are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms. They both use chemical messengers (neurotransmitters and hormones, respectively) to communicate with target cells or organs. However, they differ significantly in their composition, with the nervous system being made of neuron cells and the endocrine system composed of glands. Additionally, the nervous system transmits signals through electrochemical pulses, while the endocrine system uses hormones and relies on the circulatory system for signal transmission. The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, whereas the endocrine system's organs are discrete and not physically connected.\", \"Similarities:\\n1. Both systems are devoted to maintaining homeostasis.\\n2. They are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms.\\n3. Chemical messengers are important in both systems.\\n\\nDifferences:\\n1. Structure: The nervous system is made up of neuron cells, while the Endocrine System is made up of glands.\\n2. Transmission: The nervous system uses electrochemical pulses, whereas the Endocrine System uses hormones.\\n3. Connection: The nervous system is continuous and interconnected, while the organs of the Endocrine System are discrete and not physically connected.\\n4. Signal transmission: The nervous system utilizes neurons for signal transmission, while the Endocrine System uses the circulatory system for signal transmission.\\n\\nAnswer:[Similarities: Both systems maintain homeostasis, are regulated by negative feedback mechanisms, and use chemical messengers. Differences: The nervous system is made of neuron cells, uses electrochemical pulses for transmission, is continuous and interconnected, and utilizes neurons for signal transmission. The Endocrine System is made of glands, uses hormones for transmission, is discrete and not physically connected, and uses the circulatory system for signal transmission.]\"]", "time_sec": 39.987, "generation_time_seconds": 39.9817, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0005, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [34402, 1361, 510, 16, 13, 11733, 5942, 10306, 2114, 535, 10718, 624, 17, 13, 2379, 525, 34219, 553, 8225, 11055, 23783, 624, 18, 13, 35324, 9435, 14886, 525, 2989, 304, 2176, 5942, 382, 35, 58326, 510, 16, 13, 576, 22596, 1849, 374, 1865, 705, 315, 48284, 7761, 11, 1393, 279, 835, 77638, 1849, 374, 1865, 705, 315, 81275, 624, 17, 13, 47025, 304, 279, 22596, 1849, 374, 10660, 553, 2