{"id": "199", "question": "what causes necrotizing fasciitis", "gt": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused primarily by bacteria, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci, also known as Streptococcus pyogenes. This is the same bacteria that causes strep throat, impetigo, and rheumatic fever. Other bacteria types, such as staphylococcus, are also associated with the disease. The bacteria infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin. Prior trauma, such as a cut or scrape, insect bite, burn, or needle puncture wound can serve as a source of infection as well as surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures. In rare cases, fungi can lead to this condition.", "pred": "Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, including group A Streptococcus, staphylococcus, and others. It can also be caused by trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds, and surgical sites. In rare cases, milder infections such as strep throat and impetigo can lead to necrotizing fasciitis.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Based on the provided passages, the causes of necrotizing fasciitis include:\n\n1. Bacteria: Necrotizing fasciitis is commonly caused by group A Streptococcus (GAS) bacteria, which is also responsible for strep throat. Other types of bacteria such as staphylococcus and others have also been associated with the disease.\n\n2. Trauma: Many cases of necrotizing fasciitis are caused by prior trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds. These lesions may initially appear trivial or minor.\n\n3. Surgical sites: Surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures may also serve as a source of infection.\n\n4. Infections: The bacteria that cause necrotizing fasciitis can also cause milder infections such as strep throat and impetigo. However, in rare cases, these infections can become more dangerous and lead to necrotizing fasciitis.\n\nAnswer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, including group A Streptococcus, staphylococcus, and others. It can also be caused by trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds, and surgical sites. In rare cases, milder infections such as strep throat and impetigo can lead to necrotizing fasciitis.", "cot": "[\"Based on the provided passages, the causes of necrotizing fasciitis include:\\n\\n1. Bacteria: Necrotizing fasciitis is commonly caused by group A Streptococcus (GAS) bacteria, which is also responsible for strep throat. Other types of bacteria such as staphylococcus and others have also been associated with the disease.\\n\\n2. Trauma: Many cases of necrotizing fasciitis are caused by prior trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds. These lesions may initially appear trivial or minor.\\n\\n3. Surgical sites: Surgical incision sites and various surgical procedures may also serve as a source of infection.\\n\\n4. Infections: The bacteria that cause necrotizing fasciitis can also cause milder infections such as strep throat and impetigo. However, in rare cases, these infections can become more dangerous and lead to necrotizing fasciitis.\\n\\nAnswer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, including group A Streptococcus, staphylococcus, and others. It can also be caused by trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, insect bites, burns, or needle puncture wounds, and surgical sites. In rare cases, milder infections such as strep throat and impetigo can lead to necrotizing fasciitis.\", \"Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria, including group A Streptococcus (GAS) bacteria, which are also responsible for strep throat and other infections. These bacteria can infect the superficial fascia, a layer of connective tissue below the skin, leading to the condition. In some cases, other types of bacteria, such as staphylococcus, may also be associated with necrotizing fasciitis. The infection can be severe and potentially life-threatening, as it can destroy skin, fat, and the tissue covering the muscles within a very short time.\", \"To determine what causes necrotizing fasciitis, let's analyze the information provided in the passages:\\n\\n1. **Passage 1**: This passage mentions that necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, specifically group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), and other bacterial pathogens. It also notes that prior trauma, such as cuts, scrapes, burn, or needle puncture wounds, can serve as a source of infection.\\n\\n2. **Passage 2**: This passage reiterates that necrotizing fasciitis is commonly caused by group A Streptococcus (GAS) bacteria, similar to strep throat. It also mentions that other types of bacteria, such as staphylococcus, can also be associated with the disease.\\n\\n3. **Passage 3**: This passage provides additional details, mentioning that the bacteria causing necrotizing fasciitis can also cause milder infections like strep throat and impetigo. It emphasizes that the infections can be dangerous, even though they are usually mild.\\n\\nBased on these passages, the causes of necrotizing fasciitis include:\\n\\n- **Group A Streptococcus (GAS)**: Commonly mentioned in both passages.\\n- **Staphylococcus and other bacteria**: Mentioned in Passage 2.\\n- **Trauma**: Mentioned in both Passages 1 and 3.\\n\\nTherefore, the causes of necrotizing fasciitis are bacteria, particularly group A Streptococcus and staphylococcus, and trauma.\\n\\nAnswer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, particularly group A Streptococcus (GAS) and staphylococcus, and trauma.\", \"First, I will analyze the given passages to identify the causes of necrotizing fasciitis mentioned.\\n\\nPassage 1: \\\"Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria in the vast majority of cases, though fungi can also rarely lead to this condition as well. Many cases of necrotizing fasciitis are caused by group A beta-hemolytic streptococci (Streptococcus pyogenes), either individually or along with other bacterial pathogens.\\\"\\n\\nPassage 2: \\\"Necrotizing fasciitis is commonly caused by group A Streptococcus (GAS) bacteria. That's the same type of bacteria that causes strep throat. However, several types of bacteria, such as staphylococcus and others, have also been associated with the disease.\\\"\\n\\nPassage 3: \\\"Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by several kinds of bacteria. Some of these bacteria also cause infections such as strep throat and impetigo. Usually the infections caused by these bacteria are mild. But in rare cases they can cause a more dangerous infection.\\\"\\n\\nFrom these passages, I can conclude that the main causes of necrotizing fasciitis are bacteria, specifically group A Streptococcus (GAS) and other bacterial pathogens. Additionally, there is a mention of fungi as a rare cause.\\n\\nAnswer: Necrotizing fasciitis is caused by bacteria, including group A Streptococcus (GAS), and in rare cases, fungi.\"]", "time_sec": 34.92, "generation_time_seconds": 34.9157, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0002, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 3897, 46769, 11, 279, 11137, 315, 19053, 4640, 4849, 15195, 72, 19435, 2924, 1447, 16, 13, 425, 77752, 25, 63124, 4640, 4849, 15195, 72, 19435, 374, 16626, 8881, 553, 1874, 362, 35672, 417, 509, 91311, 320, 38, 1911, 8, 23157, 11, 892, 374, 1083, 8480, 369, 5412, 79, 27591, 13, 6944, 4494, 315, 23157, 1741, 438, 357, 1342, 88, 1074, 91311, 323, 3800, 614, 1083, 1012, 5815, 448, 279, 8457, 382, 17, 13, 17298, 13416, 25, 8999, 5048, 315, 19053, 4640, 4849, 15195, 72, 19435, 525, 8881, 553, 4867, 29888, 11, 1741, 438, 15104, 11, 20843, 288, 11, 25989, 48333, 11, 43054, 11, 476, 30309, 31355, 552, 33154, 13, 4220, 62224, 1231, 15102, 4994, 35647, 476, 8922, 382, 18, 13, 70112, 6594, 25, 70112, 3625, 1816, 6594, 323, 5257, 33833, 15966, 1231, 1083