 end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle.\nStep 9: If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly.\nStep 10: Push it until there is no more give and it is secure.\nStep 11: The other end will hang freely in the air.\nStep 12: Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm.\nStep 13: This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm.\nStep 14: Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\nStep 15: Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet.\nStep 16: If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm."}], "input_len": 407, "output_tokens": 1129, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.3, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 6.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "240", "question": "how do crab traps work", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a crab trap is a large square metal cage with a system of nets inside that allows crabs to enter through a narrow slit at the top. To set the trap, the ends with lead-ins are placed in the water and the crabs are attracted to the free meal offered by the trap. The Alaska Department of Fish and Game recommends using king crab pots that range from 6 by 6 feet to 8 by 8 feet with heights between 2 and 3 feet. Over time, these mechanisms may disable the trap, preventing it from ghost fishing.", "pred": "Crab traps work by creating a barrier that allows crabs to enter but not exit. They are typically large squares about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide, with lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom. The narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot is the only entrance, ensuring that crabs must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal. The topless crab trap is recommended as it works best.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the main components of a crab trap.\n- A crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide.\n- It has lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom.\n- There is a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot, which is the only entrance into the trap.\n\nStep 2: Understand the function of the lead-ins.\n- The lead-ins are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out.\n\nStep 3: Analyze the mechanism that attracts crabs.\n- The crab pot uses a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars.\n- This system creates a barrier that allows crabs to enter but not exit.\n\nStep 4: Consider the importance of the entrance.\n- The narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot is the only entrance, ensuring that crabs must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.\n\nStep 5: Evaluate the effectiveness of the trap.\n- The topless crab trap is recommended as it works best.\n\nAnswer: Crab traps work by creating a barrier that allows crabs to enter but not exit. They are typically large squares about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide, with lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom. The narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot is the only entrance, ensuring that crabs must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal. The topless crab trap is recommended as it works best.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the main components of a crab trap.\\n- A crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide.\\n- It has lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom.\\n- There is a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot, which is the only entrance into the trap.\\n\\nStep 2: Understand the function of the lead-ins.\\n- The lead-ins are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out.\\n\\nStep 3: Analyze the mechanism that attracts crabs.\\n- The crab pot uses a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars.\\n- This system creates a barrier that allows crabs to enter but not exit.\\n\\nStep 4: Consider the importance of the entrance.\\n- The narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot is the only entrance, ensuring that crabs must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.\\n\\nStep 5: Evaluate the effectiveness of the trap.\\n- The topless crab trap is recommended as it works best.\\n\\nAnswer: Crab traps work by creating a barrier that allows crabs to enter but not exit. They are typically large squares about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide, with lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom. The narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot is the only entrance, ensuring that crabs must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal. The topless crab trap is recommended as it works best.\", \"Crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. A crab pot is a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide. Two ends have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.\", \"Crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. The crab pots are a large square about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide. Two ends have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs realize they must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.\", \"Crab traps work by using a system of nets inside a large metal cage made of wire or flat metal mesh that is reinforced with steel bars. The traps are designed to allow crabs in but not let them out. They are large square traps, about 2 feet high and 8 feet wide, with two ends that have lead-ins that angle into the trap from the sides and bottom to a narrow slit about 2 feet into the pot. This slit is the only entrance into the trap. The crabs realize they must crawl up the leads to get to their free meal.\"]", "time_sec": 22.378, "generation_time_seconds": 22.3746, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0002, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 64547, 279, 1887, 6813, 315, 264, 59412, 22862, 624, 12, 362, 59412, 3338, 374, 264, 3460, 9334, 911, 220, 17, 7541, 1550, 323, 220, 23, 7541, 6884, 624, 12, 1084, 702, 2990, 21853, 429, 9210, 1119, 279, 22862, 504, 279, 11067, 323, 5622, 624, 12, 2619, 374, 264, 15026, 78030, 911, 220, 17, 7541, 1119, 279, 3338, 11, 892, 374, 279, 1172, 19809, 1119, 279, 22862, 382, 8304, 220, 17, 25, 70894, 