.0002, -0.0, -0.0002, -0.0004, -0.1755, -0.0001], "cumulative_logprob": -45.4283, "norm_logp": -0.1389, "input_len": 389, "output_tokens": 327, "pred": "When you breathe in, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles contract, expanding the chest cavity, which allows air to be drawn into the lungs. When you breathe out, the diaphragm and intercostal muscles relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity, which forces air out."}], "input_len": 389, "output_tokens": 702, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 69.81, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "675", "question": "inventory costing weighted average", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a brief answer to your question:\n\nThe weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. The cost of goods available for sale is divided by the number of units available for sale to calculate the weighted-average cost per unit. This figure is then used to assign a cost to both ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The weighted average method is commonly used when inventory items are intermingled and cannot be assigned a specific cost, or when the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.", "pred": "Inventory costing weighted average is a method that assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold, using the average cost of all currently available inventories. This method adjusts the average cost with fluctuations in inventory prices, resulting in inventory values that represent a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To answer the question \"What is inventory costing weighted average?\" based on the provided passages, let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. The weighted average method of costing inventory is described in the passages as a method that assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold.\n2. The average cost of all inventories currently available is taken when a unit is sold.\n3. This average price is then assigned to the item sold.\n4. The weighted average method accounts for the fluctuation in inventory prices, adjusting the average cost accordingly.\n5. It is explained that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\n\nBased on these points, the weighted average method of inventory costing involves:\n\n- Assigning an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold.\n- Using the average cost of all currently available inventories.\n- Adjusting the average cost with fluctuations in inventory prices.\n- Recording inventory values between the oldest and newest units.\n\nAnswer: Inventory costing weighted average is a method that assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold, using the average cost of all currently available inventories. This method adjusts the average cost with fluctuations in inventory prices, resulting in inventory values that represent a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.", "cot": "[\"To answer the question \\\"What is inventory costing weighted average?\\\" based on the provided passages, let's break it down step by step:\\n\\n1. The weighted average method of costing inventory is described in the passages as a method that assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold.\\n2. The average cost of all inventories currently available is taken when a unit is sold.\\n3. This average price is then assigned to the item sold.\\n4. The weighted average method accounts for the fluctuation in inventory prices, adjusting the average cost accordingly.\\n5. It is explained that the recorded amount of inventory on hand represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\\n\\nBased on these points, the weighted average method of inventory costing involves:\\n\\n- Assigning an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold.\\n- Using the average cost of all currently available inventories.\\n- Adjusting the average cost with fluctuations in inventory prices.\\n- Recording inventory values between the oldest and newest units.\\n\\nAnswer: Inventory costing weighted average is a method that assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold, using the average cost of all currently available inventories. This method adjusts the average cost with fluctuations in inventory prices, resulting in inventory values that represent a value between the oldest and newest units purchased into stock.\", \"The weighted average method of inventory costing assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This method takes the average cost of all inventories currently available when a unit of inventory is sold. This average price is then assigned to the item sold. Since inventory prices fluctuate, the weighted average cost will also change. The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are so intermingled that it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\", \"In the weighted average method of inventory costing, the average cost of all inventories currently available is assigned to each unit of inventory when it is sold. This method takes into account the fluctuating inventory prices. When a unit is sold, the weighted average cost is determined by dividing the total cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale. This results in a cost that represents a value somewhere between the oldest and newest units purchased. The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where inventory items are intermingled and it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit, or where the accounting system is not sophisticated enough to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers. Retailers and other businesses that keep and sell inventory must keep track of the cost of inventory on hand as well as the cost of inventory that was sold.\", \"In this situation, the question is asking about the weighted average method of inventory costing. This method assigns an average cost to each piece of inventory when it is sold. This average cost is calculated by dividing the cost of goods available for sale by the number of units available for sale, resulting in a weighted-average cost per unit. This average cost is then used to assign a cost to both the ending inventory and the cost of goods sold. The weighted average method is commonly used in situations where it is impossible to assign a specific cost to an individual unit or when the accounting system is not sufficiently sophisticated to track FIFO or LIFO inventory layers.\"]", "time_sec": 32.212, "generation_time_seconds": 32.1992, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0005, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 4226, 279, 3405, 330, 3838, 374, 15444, 53724, 36824, 5461, 7521, 3118, 389, 279, 3897, 46769, 11, 1077, 594, 1438, 432, 1495, 3019, 553, 3019, 1447, 16, 13, 576, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 53724, 15444, 374, 7481, 304, 279, 46769, 438, 264, 1714, 429, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 624, 17, 13, 576, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 17023, 2433, 5023, 2500, 374, 4429, 979, 264, 4982, 374, 6088, 624, 18, 13, 1096, 5461, 3349, 374, 1221, 12607, 311, 279, 1509, 6088, 624, 19, 13, 576, 36824, 5461, 1714, 9618, 369, 279, 38288, 4002, 304, 15444, 7576, 11, 42368, 279, 5461, 2783, 27079, 624, 20, 13, 1084, 374, 11247, 429, 279, 12433, 3311, 315, 15444, 389, 1424, 10868, 264, 897, 14696, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 14733, 1119, 5591, 382, 28715, 389, 1493, 3501, 11, 279, 36824, 5461, 1714, 315, 15444, 53724, 17601, 1447, 12, 31639, 287, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 624, 12, 12091, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 5023, 2500, 17023, 2433, 624, 12, 27196, 287, 279, 5461, 2783, 448, 64549, 304, 15444, 7576, 624, 12, 60547, 15444, 2750, 1948, 279, 23513, 323, 23601, 8153, 382, 16141, 25, 28126, 53724, 36824, 5461, 374, 264, 1714, 429, 49912, 458, 5461, 2783, 311, 1817, 6573, 315, 15444, 979, 432, 374, 6088, 11, 1667, 279, 5461, 2783, 315, 678, 5023, 2500, 17023, 2433, 13, 1096, 171