{"id": "172", "question": "how to soften sugar", "gt": "To soften sugar, place brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil. Cover tightly. Remove damp paper towel in a couple of days. Alternatively, preheat oven to lowest temperature (150-200 degrees) and remove sugar from package. Put in an ovenproof container and place in the warm oven for 15 minutes. Tap sugar with a spoon and if it starts to fall apart, turn oven off and leave sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Store granulated sugar in an airtight container and confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location.", "pred": "To soften sugar, use brown sugar by placing it in a plastic container with a damp paper towel on top, covering it tightly, and removing the paper towel after a couple of days. For granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature, place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and tap it with a spoon after 15 minutes. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To soften sugar, you can use the following methods:\n\n1. **Using Brown Sugar:**\n   - Place brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar.\n   - Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil.\n   - Cover tightly.\n   - Remove the damp paper towel in a couple of days.\n\n2. **Using Granulated Sugar:**\n   - Preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees).\n   - Remove sugar from the package and put it in an ovenproof container that will hold the sugar.\n   - Place in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes.\n   - Tap the sugar with a spoon.\n   - If the sugar starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out.\n   - Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.\n   - Store confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location (not the refrigerator).\n\n3. **Storing to Keep Soft:**\n   - To keep brown sugar soft, the most efficient way is to keep it sealed in an airtight container.\n   - The trick is to limit the amount of air exposure as much as possible.\n   - Pack the sugar into the smallest container possible so that there is hardly any air space between the sugar and the lid.\n\nAnswer: To soften sugar, use brown sugar by placing it in a plastic container with a damp paper towel on top, covering it tightly, and removing the paper towel after a couple of days. For granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature, place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and tap it with a spoon after 15 minutes. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.", "cot": "[\"To soften sugar, you can use the following methods:\\n\\n1. **Using Brown Sugar:**\\n   - Place brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar.\\n   - Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil.\\n   - Cover tightly.\\n   - Remove the damp paper towel in a couple of days.\\n\\n2. **Using Granulated Sugar:**\\n   - Preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees).\\n   - Remove sugar from the package and put it in an ovenproof container that will hold the sugar.\\n   - Place in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes.\\n   - Tap the sugar with a spoon.\\n   - If the sugar starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out.\\n   - Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.\\n   - Store confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location (not the refrigerator).\\n\\n3. **Storing to Keep Soft:**\\n   - To keep brown sugar soft, the most efficient way is to keep it sealed in an airtight container.\\n   - The trick is to limit the amount of air exposure as much as possible.\\n   - Pack the sugar into the smallest container possible so that there is hardly any air space between the sugar and the lid.\\n\\nAnswer: To soften sugar, use brown sugar by placing it in a plastic container with a damp paper towel on top, covering it tightly, and removing the paper towel after a couple of days. For granulated sugar, preheat the oven to the lowest temperature, place the sugar in an ovenproof container, and tap it with a spoon after 15 minutes. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.\", \"To soften sugar, you can follow these steps based on the provided passages:\\n\\n1. For brown sugar:\\n   - Place the brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar.\\n   - Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil.\\n   - Cover tightly.\\n   - Remove the damp paper towel in a couple of days.\\n\\n2. For granulated sugar:\\n   - Preheat the oven to the lowest temperature, 150-200 degrees.\\n   - Remove the sugar from the package and place it in an ovenproof container that will hold the sugar.\\n   - Place the sugar in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes.\\n   - Tap the sugar with a spoon.\\n   - If the sugar starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out.\\n   - Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.\\n   - Store confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location (not the refrigerator).\\n   - When it gets moist, it develops lumps.\\n\\nAnswer: To soften sugar, you can use damp paper towels to maintain moisture for brown sugar or use the oven method to soften granulated sugar.\", \"To soften sugar, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Granulated Sugar:**\\n   - Preheat your oven to the lowest temperature, between 150-200 degrees.\\n   - Remove the sugar from its package and place it in an ovenproof container that can hold the sugar.\\n   - Place the container in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes.\\n   - Tap the sugar with a spoon to help it soften. If the sugar starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out.\\n\\n2. **Brown Sugar:**\\n   - Place brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar.\\n   - Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil.\\n   - Cover tightly.\\n   - Remove the damp paper towel in a couple of days to allow the sugar to absorb the moisture and become soft.\\n\\nAlways store granulated sugar in an airtight container and confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location to prevent them from becoming moist and developing lumps.\", \"To soften sugar, you can follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Granulated Sugar**: Preheat your oven to the lowest temperature (150-200 degrees). Place the sugar in an ovenproof container and put it in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes. Tap the sugar with a spoon to help it soften. If it starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container and keep it in a cool, dry location (not the refrigerator).\\n\\n2. **Brown Sugar**: Keep brown sugar sealed in an airtight container to limit air exposure. Pack the sugar into the smallest container possible so that there is hardly any air space between the sugar and the lid. This helps maintain its softness.\\n\\nBy following these methods, you can effectively soften both granulated and brown sugar.\"]", "time_sec": 37.064, "generation_time_seconds": 37.0583, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 79945, 13163, 11, 498, 646, 990, 279, 2701, 5413, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 16429, 10471, 30919, 25, 1019, 256, 481, 10771, 13876, 13163, 304, 264, 12188, 5476, 448, 264, 2613, 6573, 315, 12188, 15061, 476, 46399, 916, 279, 13163, 624, 256, 481, 10224, 264, 40269, 5567, 42613, 476, 25859, 7989, 389, 1909, 