rob": -56.3646, "norm_logp": -0.1911, "input_len": 491, "output_tokens": 295, "pred": "Elements are composed of only one type of atom, compounds are composed of two or more different elements and are pure substances, and mixtures are a physical combination of elements or compounds where the parts of the mixture are not chemically bonded and retain their own identity."}], "input_len": 491, "output_tokens": 821, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.0, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 7.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "225", "question": "how to fix lift valve in toilet", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's how to fix a lift valve in a toilet:\n\n1. Shut off the water supply valve to the toilet before starting the repair (Passage 1).\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms, and remove any remaining broken lift chain from the lift arm (Passage 1).\n3. Open the water valve to fill the tank and flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If necessary, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links (Passage 2).\n4. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm, which is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm (Passage 3). Secure it using pliers to ensure it is tightly fastened.\n5. Turn the water valve back on and test the toilet's flush function. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm (Passage 3).\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages. The passages do not provide instructions on how to fix a specific issue with the lift valve, such as a stuck or leaking valve.", "pred": "1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what's left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n3. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air.\n4. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\n5. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\n6. Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you're satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To fix a lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\n\n1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what's left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\n3. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air.\n4. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\n5. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\n6. Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you're satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.", "cot": "[\"To fix a lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what's left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\\n3. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air.\\n4. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\\n5. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\\n6. Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you're satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\", \"To fix a lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Shutoff the Water Supply**: Turn off the water supply valve to the toilet to prevent water from flowing into the tank.\\n2. **Remove the Old Flapper Valve**: Carefully remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Ensure you remove any broken lift chain from the lift arm.\\n3. **Install the New Flapper Valve**: Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end of the lift arm will hang freely in the air.\\n4. **Connect the Chain**: Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm, which is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\\n5. **Turn the Water Valve Back On**: Turn the water valve back on.\\n6. **Test the Flush**: Test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\\n7. **Check for Correct Operation**: Open the water valve to fill the tank and flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. Cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can effectively fix and install a new lift valve in your toilet.\", \"To fix the lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\\n3. Open the water valve to fill the tank.\\n4. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation.\\n5. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links.\\n6. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\\n7. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly.\\n8. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air.\\n9. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm.\\n10. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\\n11. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet.\\n12. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\\n\\nAnswer:[Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm. Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.]\", \"To fix a lift valve in a toilet, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Shutoff the water supply valve to the toilet.\\n2. Remove the old flapper valve by slipping it off the mounting arms. Remove what’s left of the broken lift chain from the lift arm.\\n3. Push the small solid end of the toilet lift arm into the flush handle. If a screw is included with your part, screw it in tightly. Push it until there is no more give and it is secure. The other end will hang freely in the air.\\n4. Connect the chain to the third hole on the lift arm. This is the hole closest to the edge of the lift arm. Use the pliers to make sure it is securely fastened.\\n5. Turn the water valve back on and test out the flush of the toilet. If the toilet flushes slowly, adjust the chain to make it a little tighter on the lift arm.\\n6. Open the water valve to fill the tank. Flush the toilet to check for correct operation. If needed, readjust the lift chain by moving the metal clip up or down a couple of links. When you’re satisfied, cut off the excess lift chain to prevent it from getting tangled.\\n\\nAnswer:[To fix the lift valve in the toilet, first shutoff the water supply valve, remove the old flapper valve, and disconnect the broken lift chain. Push the lift arm into the flush handle, secure it, connect the chain to the third hole, and turn the water valve back on to test the flush. Adjust the chain if necessary, and cut off excess chain to prevent tangling.]\"]", "time_sec": 45.097, "generation_time_seconds": 45.0914, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0005, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 5046, 264, 11893, 31430, 304, 264, 26214, 11, 1795, 1493, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 1417, 27440, 279, 3015, 8149, 31430, 311, 279, 26214, 624, 17, 13, 10783, 279, 2310, 1320, 3106, 31430, 553, 63702, 432, 1007, 279, 33639, 11715, 13, 10783, 1128, 594, 2115, 315, 279, 10865, 11893, 8781, 504, 279, 11893, 6773, 624, 18, 13, 22950, 279, 2613, 6437, 835, 315, 279, 26214, 11893, 6773, 1119, 279, 18198, 3705, 13, 141