{"id": "497", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that salary exchange is a way for employees to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. The salary that is waived is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution, minus any additional contributions. The employer then pays the corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to potentially save money through reduced national insurance contributions.", "pred": "In summary, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to a reduced salary in exchange for a pension contribution from their employer, which can lead to savings for both parties involved.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Salary exchange is a method in which an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers benefit by paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) since they pay their employees a reduced salary. Employees, on the other hand, receive pension contributions from their employer, which is often a non-cash benefit. \n\nFor example, under the Salary Exchange, the salary waived by the employee is equal to the value of their regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS (depending on the pension scheme). \n\nFor instance, for members of USS, starting from 1 October 2011, the regular contribution was 7.5% of the employee's pay. Similarly, for members of USPAS, starting from 1 August 2011, the regular contribution was 9%.\n\nIn summary, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to a reduced salary in exchange for a pension contribution from their employer, which can lead to savings for both parties involved.", "cot": "[\"Salary exchange is a method in which an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. Employers benefit by paying lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) since they pay their employees a reduced salary. Employees, on the other hand, receive pension contributions from their employer, which is often a non-cash benefit. \\n\\nFor example, under the Salary Exchange, the salary waived by the employee is equal to the value of their regular pension contribution to USS, USPAS, or SGSS (depending on the pension scheme). \\n\\nFor instance, for members of USS, starting from 1 October 2011, the regular contribution was 7.5% of the employee's pay. Similarly, for members of USPAS, starting from 1 August 2011, the regular contribution was 9%.\\n\\nIn summary, salary exchange pension contributions involve an employee agreeing to a reduced salary in exchange for a pension contribution from their employer, which can lead to savings for both parties involved.\", \"Salary exchange, also referred to as salary sacrifice, is a way for employers and employees to make pension contributions. Under this arrangement, an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. Employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary.\", \"Salary exchange pension contributions are a way for both employers and employees to save money. In this arrangement, an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This results in employers paying lower NICs as a result of paying their employees a reduced salary. For example, for members of USS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 October 2011 is 7.5% of the employee's pay; for members of USPAS, the regular contribution with effect from 1 August 2011 is 9%.\", \"Salary exchange is a method where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to save money. For instance, in the case of USS, the regular contribution for members is 7.5% of the pay, and the University would pay a corresponding amount into the USS pension scheme.\"]", "time_sec": 23.797, "generation_time_seconds": 23.7901, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0005, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [41673, 9289, 374, 264, 1714, 304, 892, 458, 9364, 32908, 311, 2968, 705, 949, 315, 862, 16107, 476, 12037, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 8160, 7171, 553, 862, 19136, 13, 1096, 26112, 6147, 2176, 22426, 323, 8256, 311, 3581, 3220, 13, 20782, 388, 8760, 553, 12515, 4722, 5055, 21674, 96004, 320, 44947, 82, 8, 2474, 807, 2291, 862, 8256, 264, 11046, 16107, 13, 43641, 11, 389, 279, 1008, 1424, 11, 5258, 27681, 19026, 504, 862, 19136, 11, 892, 374, 3545, 264, 2477, 1786, 988, 8760, 13, 4710, 2461, 3110, 11, 1212, 279, 41758, 18705, 11, 279, 16107, 81482, 553, 279, 9364, 374, 6144, 311, 279, 897, 315, 862, 5792, 27681, 18527, 311, 38508, 11, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 476, 29626, 1220, 320, 80480, 389, 279, 27681, 12859, 568, 4710, 2461, 2867, 11, 369, 3613, 315, 38508, 11, 5916, 504, 220, 16, 6527, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 572, 220, 22, 13, 20, 4, 315, 279, 9364, 594, 2291, 13, 34239, 11, 369, 3613, 315, 2274, 47, 1911, 11, 5916, 504, 220, 16, 6156, 220, 17, 15, 16, 16, 11, 279, 5792, 18527, 572, 220, 24, 34332, 641, 12126, 11, 16107, 9289, 27681, 19026, 21058, 458, 9364, 38327, 311, 264, 11046, 16107, 304, 9289, 369, 264, 27681, 18527, 504, 862, 19136, 11, 892, 646, 2990, 311, 18986, 369, 2176, 9677, 6398, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.2836, -0.0156, -1.2715, -0.0002, -1.1685, -4.0662, -0.0005, -0.2054, -0.0155, -0.0797, -0.0, -0.0632, -0.00