{"id": "646", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "To plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:\n\n1. Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each using a sharp knife.\n2. Set the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet and allow them to dry out for a cool, dark location overnight.\n3. Once the cut ends are dry, thoroughly wash the potatoes.\n4. Plant the potatoes in the ground, either whole or cut into cubes with a sprout on each cube.\n5. Space the potatoes about 6 to 8 inches apart and cover them with a layer of compost.\n6. Replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n7. Water the potatoes regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n8. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.", "pred": "By following these steps, you can successfully plant a sprouted potato, ensuring it has the best chance of growing into a healthy and productive plant.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To plant a sprouted potato, follow these detailed steps:\n\n1. **Prepare the Soil**: Use a hoe or tiller to turn the soil over in your planned planting area to a depth of 12 inches and break it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. This ensures that the soil is loose and free of obstructions, which can help the potato roots grow more easily.\n\n2. **Create Trenches**: Dig a trench that is about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. If you are planting multiple rows, space them apart by about 24 to 36 inches. This creates a channel where the potato chunks will be placed.\n\n3. **Prepare the Potato Chunks**: Cut your potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. If your potatoes are not sprouted, cut them into cubes with two or three eyes on each. This ensures that each chunk has the necessary components for successful growth.\n\n4. **Place the Potato Chunks**: Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. This allows for proper spacing and air circulation, which is essential for healthy root development. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up. The sprouts will grow upwards, so positioning them correctly helps in maximizing the use of light and nutrients.\n\n5. **Cover with Compost**: After placing the potato chunks in the trench, cover them with a layer of compost. This provides nutrients and helps to maintain soil moisture. Replace the dirt on top of the plantings, making sure to cover the compost layer adequately to protect the potato chunks and promote root growth.\n\nBy following these steps, you can successfully plant a sprouted potato, ensuring it has the best chance of growing into a healthy and productive plant.", "cot": "[\"To plant a sprouted potato, follow these detailed steps:\\n\\n1. **Prepare the Soil**: Use a hoe or tiller to turn the soil over in your planned planting area to a depth of 12 inches and break it up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. This ensures that the soil is loose and free of obstructions, which can help the potato roots grow more easily.\\n\\n2. **Create Trenches**: Dig a trench that is about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. If you are planting multiple rows, space them apart by about 24 to 36 inches. This creates a channel where the potato chunks will be placed.\\n\\n3. **Prepare the Potato Chunks**: Cut your potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. If your potatoes are not sprouted, cut them into cubes with two or three eyes on each. This ensures that each chunk has the necessary components for successful growth.\\n\\n4. **Place the Potato Chunks**: Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. This allows for proper spacing and air circulation, which is essential for healthy root development. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up. The sprouts will grow upwards, so positioning them correctly helps in maximizing the use of light and nutrients.\\n\\n5. **Cover with Compost**: After placing the potato chunks in the trench, cover them with a layer of compost. This provides nutrients and helps to maintain soil moisture. Replace the dirt on top of the plantings, making sure to cover the compost layer adequately to protect the potato chunks and promote root growth.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can successfully plant a sprouted potato, ensuring it has the best chance of growing into a healthy and productive plant.\", \"1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each.\\n2. Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet and set them in a cool, dark location overnight to dry out the cut ends.\\n3. Water the potatoes thoroughly.\\n4. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\\n5. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\\n6. Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely.\\n7. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing.\\n8. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n9. If you're going to plant more than one row, space them apart by about 24 to 36 inches.\\n10. Cut your potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\\n11. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches.\\n12. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up.\\n13. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\\n\\nAnswer: To plant a potato that has sprouted, cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, place them on a tray or cookie sheet overnight to dry, water them thoroughly, add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, prepare the soil by turning it over and digging a trench, cut the potatoes into cubes with a sprout on each, place the cubes in the trench, cover with compost, and replace the dirt on top.\", \"First, cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each. Place these chunks on a tray or cookie sheet in a cool, dark location overnight to allow the cut ends to dry out. Small potatoes can be planted whole. Water the potatoes thoroughly and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines. This mulch helps keep the soil cool and maintains a consistent moisture level. \\n\\nFor planting, turn the soil over in the planned planting area to a depth of 12 inches and break it up finely. Remove any large rocks and debris. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. If planting multiple rows, space them apart by about 24 to 36 inches. Cut your potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Ensure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes faces up. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\", \"To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the soil: Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n\\n2. Cut the potato: Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife, being careful to not break the long sprouts. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\\n\\n3. Plant the potato: Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch. Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines.\\n\\n4. Follow instructions: If you're going to plant more than one row, space them apart by about 24 to 36 inches. Cut your potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\"]", "time_sec": 60.632, "generation_time_seconds": 60.6246, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0009, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 11, 1795, 1493, 11682, 7354, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 50590, 279, 75519, 95518, 5443, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 311, 2484, 279, 17171, 916, 304, 697, 12909, 47014, 3082, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 1438, 432, 705, 60702, 13, 10783, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 1008, 33998, 1393, 45876, 287, 13, 1096, 25351, 429, 279, 17171, 374, 20174, 323, 1910, 315, 1508, 19657, 11, 892, 646, 1492, 279, 38734, 19703, 3063, 803, 6707, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 4021, 350, 6655, 288, 95518, 37969, 264, 53252, 429, 374, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 13, 1416, 498, 525, 47014, 5248, 6978, 11, 3550, 1105, 10747, 553, 911, 220, 17, 19, 311, 220, 18, 21, 14924, 13, 1096, 11450, 264, 5496, 1380, 279, 38734, 26757, 686, 387, 9099, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 50590, 279, 77603, 910, 15296, 95518, 19649, 697, 34167, 1119, 54104, 11, 911, 220, 16, 311, 220, 17, 14924, 389, 1817, 3108, 11, 9380, 264, 8151, 411, 389, 1817, 23739, 13, 14