{"id": "666", "question": "what happens when you breath in and out", "gt": "When you breathe in, your diaphragm contracts and pulls downwards, expanding the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also contract to expand the ribcage. Pressure inside the chest is reduced, causing air to be sucked into the lungs. On the other hand, when you breathe out, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, reducing the space in the chest cavity. Air rich in carbon dioxide is then forced out of the lungs and windpipe. Breathing out does not require any effort from your body unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity.", "pred": "When you breathe out, or exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, through your nose or mouth.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "When you breathe in, or inhale, your diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of your chest cavity. At the same time, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. This combination of actions reduces the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be drawn into the lungs. Additionally, the outside air pushes into your windpipe (trachea) and into your lungs.\n\nWhen you breathe out, or exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, through your nose or mouth.", "cot": "[\"When you breathe in, or inhale, your diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of your chest cavity. At the same time, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. This combination of actions reduces the pressure inside the chest, causing air to be drawn into the lungs. Additionally, the outside air pushes into your windpipe (trachea) and into your lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out, or exhale, your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity. The intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax, reducing the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, through your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe out (exhalation), your diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles between the ribs also relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth. Breathing out requires no effort from your body unless you have a lung disease or are doing physical activity.\\n\\nWhen you breathe in (inhalation), the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. The diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs.\", \"When you inhale, the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage, and the diaphragm contracts, pulling downwards to increase the volume of the chest. Pressure inside the chest is lowered, and air is sucked into the lungs. When you exhale, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward into the chest cavity, and the intercostal muscles relax to reduce the space in the chest cavity. As the space in the chest cavity gets smaller, air rich in carbon dioxide is forced out of your lungs and windpipe, and then out of your nose or mouth.\", \"When you breathe in, the diaphragm contracts and moves downward, increasing the volume of the chest cavity, while the intercostal muscles contract, expanding the ribcage. This decrease in pressure inside the chest causes air to be drawn into the lungs.\\n\\nWhen you breathe out, the diaphragm relaxes and moves upward, and the intercostal muscles relax, reducing the volume of the chest cavity. This increases the pressure inside the chest, forcing air out of the lungs and windpipe, and then out of the nose or mouth.\"]", "time_sec": 25.111, "generation_time_seconds": 25.1041, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [4498, 498, 36297, 304, 11, 476, 59437, 1574, 11, 697, 1853, 94796, 76, 17080, 11, 22266, 91270, 311, 5263, 279, 8123, 315, 697, 15138, 55329, 13, 2411, 279, 1852, 882, 11, 279, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 5116, 11, 23175, 279, 20131, 66, 424, 13, 1096, 10601, 315, 6168, 25271, 279, 7262, 4766, 279, 15138, 11, 14381, 3720, 311, 387, 14764, 1119, 279, 44174, 13, 22406, 11, 279, 4889, 3720, 40702, 1119, 697, 9956, 13768, 320, 376, 1777, 64, 8, 323, 1119, 697, 44174, 382, 4498, 498, 36297, 700, 11, 476, 56659, 1574, 11, 697, 1853, 94796, 76, 11967, 288, 323, 10797, 39772, 1119, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 576, 946, 16440, 278, 23648, 1948, 279, 55149, 1083, 11967, 11, 17719, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 13, 1634, 279, 3550, 304, 279, 15138, 55329, 5221, 9155, 11, 3720, 9080, 304, 12499, 39489, 374, 9575, 700, 315, 697, 44174, 323, 9956, 13768, 11, 1526, 697, 19142, 476, 10780, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-0.1473, -0.0245, -0.135, -0.0966, -1.3549, -3.1938, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0026, -2.5808, -0.0363, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0009, -0.1179, -0.0009, -0.0114, -0.0163, -0.0001, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -4.3875, -0.0, -1.0352, -0.2532, -2.3837, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0, 0.0, -0.923, -0.0003, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0, -0.5207, -0.0014, -0.0148, -0.1174, -0.0003, -0.0055, -0.0, -0.1142, -0.1397, -2.8987, -0.4127, -0.0336, -1.2796, -0.0083, -0.0169, -0.0002, -1.1942, -0.0002, -0.5154, -0.6096, -0.006, -0.024, -0.002, -0.8077, -0.0238, -1.8397, -0.0, -0.7124, -5.5346, 0.0, -0.1805, -0.0241, -0.0, -0.01