ken-F1": 46.97, "GAVIE-Rel": 10.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 10.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "127", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "gt": "Sure! Here's my attempt to answer your question based on the provided passages:\n\nHow do you prepare the site for building a lean-to shed?\n\nAccording to passage 1, step 1, you should prepare the site by laying down a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. This will provide a stable base for the shed's foundation. Additionally, passage 2 mentions cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids, which will serve as the foundation for the shed. Finally, passage 3 provides instructions for building the shed's floor, including cutting rim joists to a length of 6 feet. Based on these passages, it appears that preparing the site involves laying down a layer of gravel and setting the skids in place before constructing the floor.", "pred": "Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel, cutting and setting 6-foot-long 4 x 4 skids, cutting and assembling the floor frame with specific measurements, ensuring the frame is square, anchoring it with joist clip angles, attaching plywood flooring, and separately constructing and raising the walls onto the frame.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "To build a lean-to shed, one must follow these steps as detailed in the provided passages:\n\n1. **Prepare the Site**: Start by preparing the site. According to the instructions, a 4″ layer of compacted gravel should be laid down.\n\n2. **Cut and Set the Skids**: Cut two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 6 feet. These skids will serve as the base for the shed.\n\n3. **Cut and Assemble the Floor Frame**: Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists and six joists to specific lengths (7 3/4 inches and 44 3/4 inches respectively). These joists will form the floor frame. After assembling the floor frame, set it on the skids.\n\n4. **Ensure Squareness**: Check that the frame is square. This is crucial for a stable shed.\n\n5. **Anchor the Frame**: Anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles to secure it in place.\n\n6. **Attach the Flooring**: Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. This step involves securing the flooring to the joists.\n\n7. **Construct the Walls**: The walls are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. This step involves building the walls and then attaching them to the floor frame.\n\nBy following these steps, one can successfully build a lean-to shed. The dimensions provided in the passages are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but the instructions suggest that the shed can be easily expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\n\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel, cutting and setting 6-foot-long 4 x 4 skids, cutting and assembling the floor frame with specific measurements, ensuring the frame is square, anchoring it with joist clip angles, attaching plywood flooring, and separately constructing and raising the walls onto the frame.", "cot": "[\"To build a lean-to shed, one must follow these steps as detailed in the provided passages:\\n\\n1. **Prepare the Site**: Start by preparing the site. According to the instructions, a 4″ layer of compacted gravel should be laid down.\\n\\n2. **Cut and Set the Skids**: Cut two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 6 feet. These skids will serve as the base for the shed.\\n\\n3. **Cut and Assemble the Floor Frame**: Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists and six joists to specific lengths (7 3/4 inches and 44 3/4 inches respectively). These joists will form the floor frame. After assembling the floor frame, set it on the skids.\\n\\n4. **Ensure Squareness**: Check that the frame is square. This is crucial for a stable shed.\\n\\n5. **Anchor the Frame**: Anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles to secure it in place.\\n\\n6. **Attach the Flooring**: Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. This step involves securing the flooring to the joists.\\n\\n7. **Construct the Walls**: The walls are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. This step involves building the walls and then attaching them to the floor frame.\\n\\nBy following these steps, one can successfully build a lean-to shed. The dimensions provided in the passages are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, but the instructions suggest that the shed can be easily expanded in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\\n\\nAnswer: Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel, cutting and setting 6-foot-long 4 x 4 skids, cutting and assembling the floor frame with specific measurements, ensuring the frame is square, anchoring it with joist clip angles, attaching plywood flooring, and separately constructing and raising the walls onto the frame.\", \"To build a lean-to shed, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel.\\n2. Cut the two 4x4 skids to a length of 6 feet.\\n3. Set and level the skids following the shed building plans.\\n4. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to a length of 7 3/4 inches and six joists to a length of 44 3/4 inches.\\n5. Assemble the floor and set it on the skids as shown in the floor framing section of the lean-to shed plans.\\n6. Check for square, and then anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.\\n\\nFor the walls:\\n7. Construct the walls separately and then raise them onto the frame.\\n\\nThe shed is 6 feet long by 4 feet wide, and you can easily expand its dimensions in increments of 2 or 4 feet.\", \"To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4″ layer of compacted gravel. Cut the two 4 x 4 skids at 70 2/4″ and set them on the gravel, ensuring they are level following the shed building plans. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4 ” and six joists at 44 3/4″. Assemble the floor and set it on the skids as shown in the floor framing section of the lean-to shed plans, checking for square, and then anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles.\", \"Building a lean-to shed involves a series of steps. First, the foundation is prepared by laying a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Next, two 4 x 4 skids are cut to a length of 70 2/4 inches and set at the site. Two 2 x 6 rim joists are then cut to a length of 7 3/4 inches, and six joists are cut to a length of 44 3/4 inches. These materials are assembled to form the floor, which is then set on the skids. The floor is checked for square, and then anchored to the skids using four joist clip angles. The walls of the shed are constructed separately and raised onto the frame.\"]", "time_sec": 30.609, "generation_time_seconds": 30.606, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [1249, 1936, 264, 15651, 4686, 24354, 11, 825, 1969, 1795, 1493, 7354, 438, 11682, 304, 279, 3897, 46769, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 50590, 279, 12911, 95518, 5145, 553, 20045, 279, 2747, 13, 10548, 311, 279, 11221, 11, 264, 220, 19, 21580, 6193, 315, 16830, 291, 41523, 1265, 387, 17113, 1495, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 37666, 323, 2573, 279, 4818, 3365, 95518, 19649, 1378, 220, 19, 856, 220, 19, 1901, 3365, 311, 264, 3084, 315, 220, 21, 7541, 13, 4220, 1901, 3365, 686, 8683, 438, 279, 2331, 369, 279, 24354, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 37666, 323, 1634, 15790, 279, 16581, 16321, 95518, 19649, 1378, 220, 17, 856, 220, 21, 30130, 7647, 1671, 323, 4743, 7647, 1671, 311, 3151, 28316, 320, 22, 220, 18, 14, 19, 14924, 323, 220, 19, 19, 220, 18, 14, 19, 14924, 15576, 568, 4220, 7647, 1671, 686, 1352, 279, 6422, 4034, 13, 4636, 80659, 279, 6422, 4034, 11, 738, 432, 389, 279, 1901, 3365, 382, 19, 13, 3070, 64439, 15619, 2090, 95518, 4248, 429, 279, 4034, 374, 9334, 13, 1096, 374, 16587, 369, 264, 15175, 24354, 382, 20, 13, 3070, 14677, 279, 16321, 95518, 48772, 279, 4034, 311, 279, 1901, 3365, 1667, 3040, 7647, 380, 12327, 25941, 311, 9767, 432, 304, 1992, 382, 21, 13, 3070, 30485, 279, 77958, 95518, 48384,