{"id": "204", "question": "what is chard vegetable", "gt": "Chard, also known as Swiss chard, is a vegetable with large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. It has a flavor that is stronger and more assertive than spinach, and some people find it bitter. Chard can be eaten raw in salads when it's young, but mature leaves and stalks are usually cooked or sautéed. Chard is actually a type of beet that is grown for its leaves rather than its root. It can produce white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. Chard is highly perishable and should be kept in the refrigerator for only 2 to 3 days.", "pred": "Chard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but tends to have a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, where its bitterness fades, leaving a refined flavor that is more delicate than that of cooked spinach. Chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production, resulting in fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. It is also an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be kept in the refrigerator for storage, unwashed leaves should be stored in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Step 1: Identify the main characteristics of chard as described in the passages.\n- Large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves\n- Thick, crisp stalks\n- Similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor\n- Can be used raw in salads\n- Mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked\n- Bitterness fades with cooking\n- Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation\n- Available in white, yellow, or red leaf stalks\n- Attractive ornamental plant\n- Extremely perishable\n\nStep 2: Synthesize the information to form a comprehensive answer.\nChard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but tends to have a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, where its bitterness fades, leaving a refined flavor that is more delicate than that of cooked spinach. Chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production, resulting in fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. It is also an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be kept in the refrigerator for storage, unwashed leaves should be stored in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.\n\nFinal Answer: Chard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but tends to have a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, where its bitterness fades, leaving a refined flavor that is more delicate than that of cooked spinach. Chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production, resulting in fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. It is also an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be kept in the refrigerator for storage, unwashed leaves should be stored in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.", "cot": "[\"Step 1: Identify the main characteristics of chard as described in the passages.\\n- Large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves\\n- Thick, crisp stalks\\n- Similar to spinach but with a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor\\n- Can be used raw in salads\\n- Mature chard leaves and stalks are typically cooked\\n- Bitterness fades with cooking\\n- Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production at the expense of storage root formation\\n- Available in white, yellow, or red leaf stalks\\n- Attractive ornamental plant\\n- Extremely perishable\\n\\nStep 2: Synthesize the information to form a comprehensive answer.\\nChard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but tends to have a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, where its bitterness fades, leaving a refined flavor that is more delicate than that of cooked spinach. Chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production, resulting in fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. It is also an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be kept in the refrigerator for storage, unwashed leaves should be stored in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Chard is a leafy green vegetable that is closely related to beets. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but tends to have a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor. It can be used raw in salads or cooked, where its bitterness fades, leaving a refined flavor that is more delicate than that of cooked spinach. Chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production, resulting in fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks. It is also an attractive ornamental plant that adds color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable, so it should be kept in the refrigerator for storage, unwashed leaves should be stored in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.\", \"Step 1: Identify the key information about chard from the passages.\\n- Chard is also known as Swiss chard.\\n- It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks.\\n- Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor.\\n- Fresh young chard can be used raw in salads.\\n- Mature chard is typically cooked.\\n- Chard is a beet that has been chosen for leaf production.\\n- Chard can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity.\\n- It is extremely perishable and should be stored in plastic bags in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.\\n\\nStep 2: Determine the main characteristics of chard as a vegetable.\\n- Chard is a vegetable with leafy greens.\\n- It has a stronger, more assertive flavor compared to spinach.\\n- It can be eaten raw or cooked.\\n- It is a type of beet that has been cultivated for leaf production.\\n\\nStep 3: Formulate the answer based on the identified characteristics.\\nChard is a leafy green vegetable that is a type of beet. It has large, fleshy but tender deep green leaves and thick, crisp stalks. Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive flavor. It can be eaten raw in salads or cooked, and it is extremely perishable.\", \"Step 1: Identify the key information in the passage about chard.\\n\\n- Chard is a beet.\\n- It is cultivated for leaf production.\\n- It can produce fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.\\n- Chard is an attractive ornamental that adds color to the vegetable garden.\\n- It can be harvested while the leaves are young and tender or after maturity when larger have slightly tougher stems.\\n- Chard is extremely perishable and should be kept refrigerated for 2 to 3 days.\\n\\nStep 2: Formulate an answer based on the information provided.\\n\\nChard is a type of beet vegetable known for its leaf production. It can produce fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks and is cultivated for ornamental purposes, adding color to the vegetable garden. Chard is extremely perishable and should be kept refrigerated for 2 to 3 days.\\n\\nAnswer: Chard is a beet vegetable known for its leaf production, with options for fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks, and is cultivated for ornamental purposes, adding color to the vegetable garden. It is extremely perishable and should be kept refrigerated for 2 to 3 days.\", \"Step 1: Identify the key characteristics of chard from the provided passage.\\n- Chard is a beet that has been cultivated for leaf production.\\n- It produces fresh white, yellow, or red leaf stalks.\\n- Chard is an attractive ornamental vegetable that adds color to the garden.\\n- It is extremely perishable and should be stored in the crisper for 2 to 3 days.\\n\\nStep 2: Determine the type of vegetable based on the information.\\n- Chard is a leafy green vegetable, as it is grown for its leaf production.\\n\\nStep 3: Compare chard to other vegetables mentioned in the passage.\\n- Chard is similar to spinach but has a stronger, more assertive (some think, bitter) flavor.\\n- It is also related to beetroot and other beet cultivars.\\n\\nStep 4: Conclude the type of vegetable based on the information.\\n- Chard is a leafy green vegetable, specifically a type of beet that is grown for its leaf production.\\n\\nFinal Answer: Chard is a leafy green vegetable, specifically a type of beet that is grown for its leaf production.\"]", "time_sec": 41.722, "generation_time_seconds": 41.7184, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0006, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [8304, 220, 16, 25, 64547, 279, 1887, 17452, 315, 521, 567, 438, 7481, 304, 279, 46769, 624, 12, 20286, 11, 282, 642, 8503, 714, 27582, 5538, 6176, 10901, 198, 12, 81277, 11, 41854, 54872, 82, 198, 12, 21476, 311, 67698, 714, 448, 264, 16245, 11, 803, 2060, 533, 320, 14689, 1744, 11, 25180, 8, 17172, 198, 12, 2980, 387, 1483, 7112, 304, 76752, 198, 12, 42159, 521, 567, 10901, 323, 54872, 82, 525, 11136, 29105, 198, 12, 425, 3248, 2090, 86409, 448, 17233, 198, 12, 910, 567, 374, 264, 48012, 429, 702, 1012, 11882, 369, 15933, 5670, 518, 279, 20284, 315, 5819, 3704, 18003, 198, 12, 16136, 304, 4158, 11, 137