-0.0025, -0.007, -0.0005, -0.8317, -0.0, -0.6344, -0.0304, -0.0538, -0.1747, -0.0091, 0.0, -0.9306, -0.0882, -0.5352, -0.0, -0.252, -0.0016, -0.1214, -0.0108, -3.1723, -0.0018, -0.0875, -0.0, -0.0254, -0.4348, -0.0046, -0.0003, -0.1016, -0.0371], "cumulative_logprob": -19.7466, "norm_logp": -0.2821, "input_len": 361, "output_tokens": 70, "pred": "Examples of potential energy include the energy stored in an object due to its vertical position or height, as in the case of a satellite in orbit or a ball on top of a hill. Examples of kinetic energy include the energy possessed by a moving object, such as a car in motion, a ball rolling down a hill, or an athlete running."}, {"rollout": 2, "token_ids": [40381, 315, 4650, 4802, 2924, 279, 4802, 9768, 304, 458, 1633, 4152, 311, 1181, 2309, 476, 2608, 11, 1741, 438, 264, 23210, 304, 26507, 476, 264, 4935, 389, 264, 23946, 13, 25311, 315, 70323, 4802, 2924, 279, 4802, 315, 11379, 11, 1741, 438, 264, 1803, 7218, 11, 264, 4935, 20097, 11, 476, 458, 33780, 4303, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.4119, -0.0006, -0.4676, -0.0083, -0.0411, -0.3646, -0.0192, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.1924, -0.0001, -0.5972, 0.0, -0.0, -1.91, -0.0921, -0.0017, -0.0773, -0.0995, 0.0, -0.5033, -0.7861, -0.0019, -0.0, -0.4576, -0.2232, -0.0601, -0.8473, -1.8454, -1.2686, -0.0112, -0.0752, -0.0, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0018, -0.0032, -0.0004, -1.7286, -0.1818, -0.1608, -0.0032, 0.0, -0.2382, -0.0973, -0.0157, -0.1748, -0.0004, -0.0017, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.2547, -0.3625, -0.0004, -0.0, -0.03, -0.0167], "cumulative_logprob": -14.6362, "norm_logp": -0.2568, "input_len": 361, "output_tokens": 57, "pred": "Examples of potential energy include the energy stored in an object due to its position or height, such as a satellite in orbit or a ball on a hill. Examples of kinetic energy include the energy of motion, such as a car moving, a ball rolling, or an athlete running."}, {"rollout": 3, "token_ids": [95611, 12354, 25, 1527, 3110, 315, 4650, 4802, 374, 279, 4802, 9768, 304, 458, 1633, 438, 279, 1102, 315, 1181, 12140, 2309, 476, 2608, 13, 30891, 5298, 12354, 25, 25311, 2924, 279, 70323, 4802, 315, 458, 42924, 304, 10971, 4152, 311, 1181, 3460, 3072, 323, 4937, 15435, 11, 323, 279, 70323, 4802, 315, 264, 19507, 14989, 553, 264, 40970, 4152, 311, 1181, 4937, 15435, 13, 151645], "logp_list": [-1.3078, -0.5953, -1.5446, -3.1048, -0.2875, -0.0172, -0.0355, 0.0, -0.0351, -0.3739, -0.0716, -0.0024, -0.0, -0.3034, -0.0001, -0.8058, -0.0431, 0.0, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0014, -0.0, -0.0003, 0.0, -0.5134, -0.7501, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0001, -0.2884, -1.0543, -1.797, -1.3466, 0.0, -0.0054, -0.024, -0.0154, -0.0368, -0.0, -0.3737, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0647, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0006, -0.0, -0.0026, -0.0406, -0.0656, -0.0017, 0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0004, -0.0027, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.4313, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0646, -0.0, -0.4851, -0.0065], "cumulative_logprob": -15.902, "norm_logp": -0.2409, "input_len": 361, "output_tokens": 66, "pred": "Potential Energy: An example of potential energy is the energy stored in an object as the result of its vertical position or height. Kinetic Energy: Examples include the kinetic energy of an airplane in flight due to its large mass and fast velocity, and the kinetic energy of a baseball thrown by a pitcher due to its fast velocity."}], "input_len": 361, "output_tokens": 484, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.51, "GAVIE-Rel": 9.0, "GAVIE-Acc": 9.5, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "304", "question": "what causes watermelons to explode", "gt": "Watermelons can explode due to external pressure, such as when a bunch of rubber bands are placed around the middle of the watermelon, causing potential energy to build up and eventually cause the watermelon to explode. Another cause of watermelon explosions is the buildup of gas inside the watermelon due to rotting. This increases the internal pressure and when the skin of the watermelon can no longer hold the gas inside, it will explode.", "pred": "The watermelon explodes due to the buildup of gas inside the watermelon as it decays. This increased pressure causes the skin of the watermelon to become unable to hold the gas inside, leading to an explosive release.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "The watermelon explodes due to the buildup of gas inside the watermelon as it decays. This increased pressure causes the skin of the watermelon to become unable to hold the gas inside, leading to an explosive release.", "cot": "[\"The watermelon explodes due to the buildup of gas inside the watermelon as it decays. This increased pressure causes the skin of the watermelon to become unable to hold the gas inside, leading to an explosive release.\", \"Step 1: Analyze the given passages.\\nThe passages discuss the concept of potential energy and the conditions that lead to an exploding watermelon. They mention rubber bands as a source of potential energy, which can cause the watermelon to explode when the pressure builds up.\\n\\nStep 2: Identify the key information.\\n- Rubber bands are a source of potential energy.\\n- The pressure from outside the watermelon can cause it to explode.\\n- The gas inside the watermelon tries to escape.\\n- As the gas builds up due to rotting, the pressure increases.\\n- When the skin of the watermelon is no longer strong enough to hold the gas, it will explode.\\n\\nStep 3: Formulate the answer based on the key information.\\nWatermelons can explode due to the buildup of pressure from both inside and outside the watermelon. The inside pressure increases as the gas inside the watermelon tries to escape and builds up due to rotting. The outside pressure is caused by the rubber bands placed around the middle of the watermelon, which creates potential energy that is released when the watermelon explodes. The skin of the watermelon is not strong enough to hold the increased pressure, causing it to explode.\\n\\nAnswer: Watermelons can explode due to the buildup of pressure from both inside and outside the watermelon, caused by the rotting of the watermelon and the placement of rubber bands around the middle of the watermelon.\", \"Step 1: Identify the key points in the passage about watermelon explosions.\\n- The rubber bands around the watermelon create potential energy.\\n- The gas inside the watermelon slowly increases due to rotting.\\n- The pressure builds up until the skin of the watermelon is no longer strong enough to contain it.\\n\\nStep 2: Combine these points to form a comprehensive answer.\\nWatermelons explode due to the accumulation of gas inside them as they rot. The gas builds up pressure, which eventually exceeds the strength of the watermelon's skin, causing it to explode.\\n\\nAnswer: Watermelons explode due to the accumulation of gas inside them as they rot, which builds up pressure until the skin of the watermelon is no longer strong enough to contain it.\", \"The watermelon explodes due to the build-up of gas inside it as a result of the rotting process. This pressure causes the watermelon's skin to no longer be strong enough to hold the gas inside, leading to the explosion.\"]", "time_sec": 25.929, "generation_time_seconds": 25.9241, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0003, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [785, 3015, 71118, 93913, 4152, 311, 279, 85665, 315, 6819, 4766, 279, 3015, 71118, 438, 432, 1622, 942