{"id": "340", "question": "how to make ribs", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make ribs:\n\nTo grill the ribs:\n\n1. Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium temperature (350°F to 450°F).\n2. Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce.\n3. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes.\n4. Flip and brush the ribs and cook for another 5 minutes.\n5. Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through and have grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.\n\nTo prepare the ribs before grilling:\n\n1. Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer.\n2. Season the ribs by mixing the mustard and liquid smoke (if using), and brushing the ribs on both sides.\n3. Sprinkle the ribs with the dry rub and pat gently to make sure the rub adheres to the rib meat.\n\nFor oven-cooked BBQ ribs:\n\n1. Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs.\n2. Put the ribs in a resealable bag.\n3. Add sufficient BBQ sauce to cover the ribs.\n4. Line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil.\n5. Place the ribs in the baking pan and bake at 350°F for 2-3 hours, or until the ribs are tender and the meat starts to pull away from the bone.", "pred": "1. Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer.\n2. Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs.\n3. Season the ribs by mixing mustard and liquid smoke (if using), brushing the ribs on both sides, and sprinkling with a dry rub.\n4. Put the ribs in a resealable bag and add BBQ sauce to cover.\n5. Line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil, overlapping it to make a tent.\n6. Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F).\n7. Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce.\n8. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes, then flip and brush the ribs and cook another 5 minutes.\n9. Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through and have grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.\n10. If oven-cooked, remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs, put them in a resealable bag with BBQ sauce, and line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil overlapping it to make a tent.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "1. Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer.\n2. Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs.\n3. Season the ribs by mixing mustard and liquid smoke (if using), brushing the ribs on both sides, and sprinkling with a dry rub.\n4. Put the ribs in a resealable bag and add BBQ sauce to cover.\n5. Line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil, overlapping it to make a tent.\n6. Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F).\n7. Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce.\n8. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes, then flip and brush the ribs and cook another 5 minutes.\n9. Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through and have grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.\n10. If oven-cooked, remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs, put them in a resealable bag with BBQ sauce, and line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil overlapping it to make a tent.", "cot": "[\"1. Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer.\\n2. Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs.\\n3. Season the ribs by mixing mustard and liquid smoke (if using), brushing the ribs on both sides, and sprinkling with a dry rub.\\n4. Put the ribs in a resealable bag and add BBQ sauce to cover.\\n5. Line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil, overlapping it to make a tent.\\n6. Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F).\\n7. Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce.\\n8. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes, then flip and brush the ribs and cook another 5 minutes.\\n9. Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through and have grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.\\n10. If oven-cooked, remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs, put them in a resealable bag with BBQ sauce, and line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil overlapping it to make a tent.\", \"To make ribs, follow these steps:\\n1. If using a grill, heat the grill to medium (350°F to 450°F). Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes. Flip and brush the ribs and cook another 5 minutes. Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through and have grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.\\n2. If using an oven, remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs. Use a knife to start it near the narrow end, and pull off in one piece. Put the ribs in a resealable bag. Add sufficient BBQ sauce to cover. Line the baking pan with heavy duty foil. Use plenty of overlapping foil to make a tent.\\n3. Season the ribs: Mix the mustard and the liquid smoke, if using, and brush the ribs on both sides. Sprinkle the ribs with the dry rub and pat gently to make sure the rub adheres to the rib meat.\\n4. Cook the ribs in the oven at 350°F for 15-20 minutes or until they are tender and have absorbed the sauce. \\n5. Once the ribs are done, remove them from the oven and let them rest for 5-10 minutes before slicing and serving.\", \"To make ribs, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Grill Method**: \\n   - Preheat the grill to medium heat (350°F to 450°F).\\n   - Remove the ribs from the foil and place them meaty-side up on the grill.\\n   - Brush them with some sauce and cover the grill to cook for 5 minutes.\\n   - Flip the ribs and brush them again, then cook for another 5 minutes.\\n   - Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened, the ribs are heated through, and have grill marks. This should take a total of 20 minutes.\\n\\n2. **Oven Method**:\\n   - Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs.\\n   - Put the ribs in a resealable bag and add enough BBQ sauce to cover them.\\n   - Line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil to make a tent.\\n   - This method does not provide specific cooking times but generally involves baking the ribs in the oven until they are tender and the sauce is flavorful.\\n\\n3. **Seasoning**:\\n   - Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer to allow for even heat circulation.\\n   - Season the ribs by mixing mustard and liquid smoke (if using) and brushing it on both sides.\\n   - Sprinkle the ribs with a dry rub and pat gently to ensure the rub adheres to the meat.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can prepare ribs using either the grill or the oven method, with careful attention to seasoning and cooking times.\", \"To make ribs, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Grill Ribs**:\\n   - Heat the grill to medium (350°F to 450°F).\\n   - Remove the ribs from the foil and place them meaty-side up on the grill.\\n   - Brush them with some sauce and cover the grill for 5 minutes.\\n   - Flip the ribs and brush them again, then cook for another 5 minutes.\\n   - Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through and have grill marks.\\n   - Total grilling time is 20 minutes.\\n\\n2. **Oven-Roast Ribs**:\\n   - Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs.\\n   - Put the ribs in a resealable bag with sufficient BBQ sauce to cover them.\\n   - Line a baking pan with heavy-duty foil, making sure it overlaps to create a tent.\\n\\n3. **Season the Ribs**:\\n   - Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer to allow for heat circulation on all sides.\\n   - Mix mustard and liquid smoke (if using) and brush the ribs on both sides.\\n   - Sprinkle the ribs with a dry rub and pat gently to ensure the rub adheres to the rib meat.\\n\\nAnswer: To make ribs, you can grill them or oven-roast them. Grilling involves heating the grill to medium, placing the ribs meaty-side up, and brushing them with sauce while flipping and cooking for 20 minutes. Oven-roasting involves removing the membrane, putting the ribs in a bag with BBQ sauce, lining a pan with foil, and seasoning them with mustard, liquid smoke, and a dry rub.\"]", "time_sec": 54.799, "generation_time_seconds": 54.792, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0009, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [16, 13, 43924, 279, 55149, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 29659, 304, 264, 3175, 6193, 624, 17, 13, 10783, 279, 38554, 504, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 55149, 624, 18, 13, 14263, 279, 55149, 553, 26792, 64307, 323, 14473, 16205, 320, 333, 1667, 701, 76726, 279, 55149, 389, 2176, 11067, 11, 323, 50698, 2718, 448, 264, 9058, 10273, 624, 19, 13, 10224, 279, 55149, 304, 264, 312, 325, 278, 480, 8968, 323, 912, 45277, 19187, 311, 3421, 624, 20, 13, 7083, 279, 27815, 7215, 448, 8811, 54518, 46399, 11, 49817, 432, 311, 1281, 264, 15632, 624, 21, 13, 26070, 264, 6819, 476, 60103, 39058, 311, 11051, 320, 18, 20, 15, 58472, 311, 220, 19, 20, 15, 58472, 4292, 22, 13, 10783, 279, 55149, 504, 279, 46399, 11, 1992, 1105, 13041, 88, 24067, 705, 389, 279, 39058, 11, 323, 15625, 1105, 448, 1045, 315, 279, 19187, 624, 23, 13, 17757, 279, 39058, 323, 4296, 369, 220, 20, 4420, 11, 1221, 18287, 323, 15625, 279, 55149, 323, 4296, 2441, 220, 20, 4420, 624, 24, 13, 15003, 311, 4296, 11, 64661, 323, 76726, 1449, 220, 20, 4420, 11, 3080, 279, 19187, 702, 12045, 6758, 323, 279, 55149, 525, 31713, 1526, 323, 614, 39058, 15423, 11, 369, 264, 2790, 1081, 9400, 882, 315, 220, 17, 15, 4420, 624, 16, 15, 13, 1416, 23387, 22471, 10823, 11, 4057, 279, 38