, -0.0491, -0.0706, -0.1794, -0.0137, -0.0812, -0.0007, -0.0293, -0.1686, -0.3054, -0.1574, -2.5826, -0.0967, -0.3114, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0071, -0.074, -0.0, -0.9336, -0.0, -0.6477, -0.0001, -0.0002, -1.2314, -0.001, 0.0, -0.0794, -0.333, -0.0187, 0.0, -0.1996, -0.0009, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0007, -0.0, -0.0217, -0.9129, -0.0001, -0.5004, -0.0, 0.0, -0.6032, -1.3952, -0.0737, -0.0157, -0.0164, 0.0, -0.0136, -0.0994, -0.0, -0.5944, 0.0, -0.0011, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.1261, -0.5767, -2.4603, -0.0087, -0.3036, -0.0159, -0.6797, -0.0, -0.0828, -0.0, -0.1125, -0.0608, -0.2272, -0.9267, -0.6138, -0.0179, 0.0, -0.0002, -0.0, -0.0, -0.1317, -1.0088, 0.0, -0.4633, -0.2122, -0.1455, -0.4806, -0.0217, -0.0009, -0.0012, -0.0239, -0.0, 0.0, -0.7, -2.2641, -0.0963, 0.0, -0.0105, -0.0, -0.0037, -0.0125, -0.9253, 0.0, -0.0432, -0.0963, -0.0889, -0.0, -0.3137, -3.5138, -0.014, -0.0068, -0.1582, -0.0003, 0.0, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, -0.0001, -0.0, 0.0, -0.0002, -0.0003, -0.0, -0.0, -0.0416, -0.0], "cumulative_logprob": -41.5153, "norm_logp": -0.2628, "input_len": 573, "output_tokens": 158, "pred": "Heredity, hormonal shifts, poor hygiene, and topical applications."}], "input_len": 573, "output_tokens": 784, "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 19.05, "GAVIE-Rel": 7.5, "GAVIE-Acc": 5.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "518", "question": "how do raffles work", "gt": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, who have the opportunity to win prizes. The specific details and mechanics of a raffle can vary. In a reverse raffle, participants purchase tickets with numbers on them, and the last ticket drawn wins the main prize. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the proceeds go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is allocated for a charitable purpose. In some cases, raffles may also offer additional opportunities to win prizes, such as cash tip board raffles.", "pred": "In summary, raffles work by selling tickets with a chance to win a prize. The specific details of how raffles are conducted can vary depending on the type of raffle and the event.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Raffles are a type of lottery game where participants purchase tickets with a chance to win a prize. The specific details of how raffles work can vary depending on the type of raffle.\n\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In a reverse raffle, the process is reversed. Instead of drawing a winning ticket, participants purchase a numbered ticket. The highest numbered ticket wins the prize. For example, if there are 55 tickets, the ticket with the number 55 would win the prize. In this type of raffle, tickets cost $50 each, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\n\n2. **50-50 Draw**: A 50-50 draw is typically held during a single event, such as a sports game or concert. Patrons buy tickets during the event, and the proceeds from these tickets go into a prize pool. At the end of the event, a random draw is held, and the winner takes half of the prize pool. The other half of the prize pool is set aside for a charitable purpose.\n\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These are typically offered at events like concerts or sports games. Participants can buy additional tickets to participate in a raffle where the prize is a cash amount (e.g., $10,000). These raffles are not included in the original ticket price.\n\nIn summary, raffles work by selling tickets with a chance to win a prize. The specific details of how raffles are conducted can vary depending on the type of raffle and the event.", "cot": "[\"Raffles are a type of lottery game where participants purchase tickets with a chance to win a prize. The specific details of how raffles work can vary depending on the type of raffle.\\n\\n1. **Reverse Raffle**: In a reverse raffle, the process is reversed. Instead of drawing a winning ticket, participants purchase a numbered ticket. The highest numbered ticket wins the prize. For example, if there are 55 tickets, the ticket with the number 55 would win the prize. In this type of raffle, tickets cost $50 each, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1000. There are two $500 prizes: one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled.\\n\\n2. **50-50 Draw**: A 50-50 draw is typically held during a single event, such as a sports game or concert. Patrons buy tickets during the event, and the proceeds from these tickets go into a prize pool. At the end of the event, a random draw is held, and the winner takes half of the prize pool. The other half of the prize pool is set aside for a charitable purpose.\\n\\n3. **Cash Tip Board Raffles**: These are typically offered at events like concerts or sports games. Participants can buy additional tickets to participate in a raffle where the prize is a cash amount (e.g., $10,000). These raffles are not included in the original ticket price.\\n\\nIn summary, raffles work by selling tickets with a chance to win a prize. The specific details of how raffles are conducted can vary depending on the type of raffle and the event.\", \"Raffles typically involve the sale of tickets for a chance to win a prize. In the context of the provided passages, a reverse raffle works as follows: Each entry ticket has a number from 1 to approximately 55, and tickets are priced at $50 each. A table consists of 20 individuals, which constitutes one complete ticket at $1000. The table has two $500 prizes (one for the early bird drawing and one for the first ticket pulled).\\n\\nThe 50-50 draw works similarly in a single-day event: Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the stadium during the event by roaming staff. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold go into a prize pool, with 50% allocated to the winner of the random draw at the end of the event, and the remaining 50% reserved for a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\\n\\nThe cash tip board raffles allow participants to win $10,000, but these are not included in the ticket price and are a separate event.\\n\\nIn summary, raffles are a form of gambling where participants buy tickets to win prizes. The reverse raffle and 50-50 draw are specific types of raffles mentioned in the passages, each with their own unique rules and prize structures.\", \"Raffles typically involve the sale of numbered tickets, a drawing of a winning ticket, and the distribution of a prize. The specific mechanics can vary, but the general process is consistent across different types of raffles.\\n\\nFor example, a traditional raffle might work as follows:\\n1. Tickets are sold to participants.\\n2. Each ticket has a unique number.\\n3. A drawing is held to determine a winning ticket.\\n4. The prize is awarded to the holder of the winning ticket.\\n\\nIn contrast, the reverse raffle mentioned in Passage 1 works in reverse:\\n1. The event organizers sell tickets, each with a number.\\n2. The tickets are grouped into a table, with 20 individuals per table.\\n3. A drawing occurs, and the winning number is determined.\\n4. The prize is awarded to the individual whose ticket corresponds to the winning number.\\n\\nThe 50-50 draw described in Passage 2 is another variation:\\n1. Tickets are sold during an event.\\n2. The proceeds from the ticket sales go into a prize pool.\\n3. A random draw is conducted at the end of the event.\\n4. 50% of the prize pool goes to the winner, and the remaining 50% is set aside for charity.\\n\\nThese examples illustrate the diversity of raffle formats, each with its own unique rules and procedures. However, the core concept remains consistent: participants purchase tickets, a winner is drawn, and a prize is awarded.\", \"Raffles typically involve the sale of tickets, with each ticket having a unique number. At a predetermined time, one or more tickets are drawn randomly. The holder of the winning ticket is the winner of the raffle. The prize money is then distributed to the winner, and often a portion may be set aside for charities or other purposes. For example, in the 50-50 draw, 50% of the proceeds go to the winner, and the remaining 50% is set aside for charitable purposes. In a reverse raffle, like the one described, tickets are more expensive, and the prizes are larger, often including cash or other valuable items.\"]", "time_sec": 46.834, "generation_time_seconds": 46.827, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [49, 80535, 525, 264, 943, 315, 38239, 1809, 1380, 13026, 7627, 14403, 448, 264, 6012, 311, 3164, 264, 21882, 13, 576, 3151, 3565, 315, 1246, 435, 80535, 975, 646, 13289, 11649, 389, 279, 943, 315, 435, 44090, 382, 16, 13, 3070, 45695, 431, 44090, 95518, 758, 264, 9931, 435, 44090, 11, 279, 1882, 374, 27437, 13, 12090, 315, 13330, 264, 10987, 11727, 11, 13026, 7627, 264, 48826, 11727, 13, 576, 8426, 48826, 11727, 14816, 279, 21882, 13, 1752, 3110, 11, 421, 1052, 525, 220, 20, 20, 14403, 11, 279, 11727, 448, 279, 1372, 220, 20, 20, 1035, 3164, 279, 21882, 13, 758, 419, 943, 315, 435, 44090, 11, 14403, 2783, 400, 20, 15, 1817, 11, 323, 264, 1965, 315, 220, 17, 15