{"id": "647", "question": "how to plant a potato that has sprouted", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to plant a potato that has sprouted:\n\n1. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, making sure to not break the long sprouts.\n2. Allow the cut ends to dry out overnight in a cool, dark location.\n3. Plant the potato chunks or whole potatoes in well-draining soil, spacing them 24 to 36 inches apart.\n4. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep, and place the potato pieces in the bottom of the trench with the sprouted side facing up.\n5. Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\n\nNote that the passages do not provide specific information on how to care for the potatoes after planting, such as watering and fertilization.", "pred": "To plant a sprouted potato, first prepare the soil by turning it over and breaking it up finely, then dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, and place them in the trench, with the sprouted side facing up. Cover with a layer of compost and replace the soil, then add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting vines. Water thoroughly and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "base_sc", "raw_output": "Based on the information provided in the given passages, here are the steps to plant a sprouted potato:\n\n1. **Prepare the Soil**: Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\n\n2. **Cut the Potato**: Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife, being careful to not break the long sprouts. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\n\n3. **Planting**: Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up.\n\n4. **Mulch and Water**: Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the area thoroughly. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\n\n5. **Mulch Around the Plants**: Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines. This helps keep the soil cool while maintaining a consistent moisture level.\n\nAnswer: To plant a sprouted potato, first prepare the soil by turning it over and breaking it up finely, then dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, and place them in the trench, with the sprouted side facing up. Cover with a layer of compost and replace the soil, then add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting vines. Water thoroughly and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.", "cot": "[\"Based on the information provided in the given passages, here are the steps to plant a sprouted potato:\\n\\n1. **Prepare the Soil**: Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n\\n2. **Cut the Potato**: Cut large, sprouted potatoes into chunks with two or three eyes each, using a sharp knife, being careful to not break the long sprouts. Small potatoes can be planted whole.\\n\\n3. **Planting**: Place the potato chunks on a tray or cookie sheet, and set them in a cool, dark location overnight so the cut ends can dry out. Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up.\\n\\n4. **Mulch and Water**: Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings. Water the area thoroughly. Continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\\n\\n5. **Mulch Around the Plants**: Add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting potato vines. This helps keep the soil cool while maintaining a consistent moisture level.\\n\\nAnswer: To plant a sprouted potato, first prepare the soil by turning it over and breaking it up finely, then dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. Cut the sprouted potato into chunks with two or three eyes each, and place them in the trench, with the sprouted side facing up. Cover with a layer of compost and replace the soil, then add a 4-inch layer of mulch around the plants, pulling it back at least 4 inches from the sprouting vines. Water thoroughly and continue watering regularly when the soil is dry to the touch.\", \"To plant a potato that has sprouted, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Prepare the Soil**: Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing.\\n\\n2. **Dig a Trench**: Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep. If you're going to plant more than one row, space them apart by about 24 to 36 inches.\\n\\n3. **Prepare the Potatoes**: Cut the potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. If the potato is small, you can plant it whole. \\n\\n4. **Plant the Potatoes**: Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Place the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up.\\n\\n5. **Cover and Replace Soil**: Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\\n\\nThis method ensures that the potato chunks are properly positioned and covered with soil, promoting healthy growth.\", \"To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. **Prepare the Soil**: Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing.\\n\\n2. **Dig a Trench**: Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n\\n3. **Cut the Potato**: Cut your potatoes into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Ensure that the nonsprouted sections have two or three eyes on each.\\n\\n4. **Plant the Cubes**: Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Position the sprouted or eye side of the cubes facing up.\\n\\n5. **Cover with Compost**: Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost.\\n\\n6. **Replace the Dirt**: Replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can plant a sprouted potato successfully.\", \"To plant a sprouted potato, follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Prepare the soil: Turn the soil over in your planned planting area with a hoe or tiller to a depth of 12 inches and break the dirt up finely. Remove any large rocks and other debris while hoeing. Dig a trench about 4 inches wide and 6 to 8 inches deep.\\n\\n2. Cut the potato: Cut the sprouted potato into cubes, about 1 to 2 inches on each side, leaving a sprout on each cube. Nonsprouted sections should have two or three eyes on each.\\n\\n3. Place the potato cubes in the trench: Place the cubes into the bottom of the trench, separating each cube from the other by about 6 to 8 inches. Make sure the sprouted or eye side of the cubes is facing up.\\n\\n4. Cover the potatoes: Cover the potatoes with a layer of compost and then replace the dirt on top of the plantings.\\n\\nNote: The passages mention that small potatoes can be planted whole, but for sprouted potatoes, the instructions are to cut them into cubes as described above.\"]", "time_sec": 49.875, "generation_time_seconds": 49.8686, "selection_time_seconds": 0.0004, "record_process": true, "trajectories": [{"rollout": 0, "token_ids": [28715, 389, 279, 1995, 3897, 304, 279, 2661, 46769, 11, 1588, 525, 279, 7354, 311, 6008, 264, 8151, 18533, 38734, 1447, 16, 13, 3070, 50590, 279, 75519, 95518, 11999, 279, 17171, 916, 304, 697, 12909, 47014, 3082, 448, 264, 45876, 476, 11956, 261, 311, 264, 7990, 315, 220, 16, 17, 14924, 323, 1438, 279, 25284, 705, 60702, 13, 10783, 894, 3460, 23035, 323, 1008, 33998, 1393, 45876, 287, 13, 37969, 264, 53252, 911, 220, 19, 14924, 6884, 323, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 5538, 382, 17, 13, 3070, 37666, 279, 77603, 95518, 19649, 3460, 11, 8151, 18533, 34167, 1119, 26757, 448, 1378, 476, 2326, 6414, 1817, 11, 1667, 264, 17232, 21430, 11, 1660, 16585, 311, 537, 1438, 279, 1293, 8151, 11672, 13, 14994, 34167, 646, 387, 38341, 4361, 382, 18, 13, 3070, 54647, 287, 95518, 10771, 279, 38734, 26757, 389, 264, 34688, 476, 12544, 10834, 11, 323, 738, 1105, 304, 264, 7010, 11, 6319, 3728, 24398, 773, 279, 3931, 10335, 646, 9058, 700, 13, 10771, 279, 54104, 1119, 279, 5622, 315, 279, 53252, 11, 49445, 1817, 23739, 504, 279, 1008, 553, 911, 220, 21, 311, 220, 23, 14924, 13, 10771, 279, 8151, 18533, 476, 7912, 3108, 315, 279, 54104, 12880, 705, 382, 19, 13, 3070, 59155, 331, 323, 9959, 95518, 17757, 279, 34167, 448, 264, 6193, 315, 56723, 323, 1221, 8290, 279, 25284, 389, 1909, 315, 279, 6008, 819, 13, 9959, 279, 3082, 26364, 13, 15003, 72741, 15502, 979, 279, 17171, 374, 9058, 311, 279, 5796, 382, 20, 13, 3070, 59155, 331, 32816, 279, 49198, 95518, 2691, 264, 220, 19, 24241, 6193, 315, 15602, 331, 2163, 279, 10779, 11, 22266, 432, 1182, 518, 3245, 220, 19,